.SH "توضیحات (DESCRIPTION)"
.sp
سیستم مرکوریال (Mercurial) از مجموعه‌ای از پرونده‌های پیکربندی برای کنترل جنبه‌های مختلف رفتار خود استفاده می‌کند.
.SH "عیب‌یابی (TROUBLESHOOTING)"
.sp
اگر با پیکربندی خود دچار مشکل شده‌اید، دستور
\%\fBhg config \-\-source\fP\:
می‌تواند به شما کمک کند تا متوجه شوید چه پرونده‌ای یک تنظیم خاص را به محیط شما وارد کرده است.
.sp
برای کسب اطلاعات درباره نحوه و مکان بازنویسی تنظیمات، به
\%\fBhg help config.syntax\fP\:
و
\%\fBhg help config.files\fP\:
مراجعه نمایید.
.SH "ساختار (STRUCTURE)"
.sp
پرونده‌های پیکربندی از ساختار ساده ini\-file استفاده می‌کنند. یک پرونده پیکربندی شامل بخش‌هایی است که با سرآیند \fB[section]\fP مشخص شده و به دنبال آن ورودی‌های \fBname = value\fP قرار می‌گیرند:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[ui]
username = Firstname Lastname <firstname.lastname@example.net>
verbose = True
.ft P
.fi
.sp
به ورودی‌های بالا به ترتیب به عنوان \fBui.username\fP و \fBui.verbose\fP اشاره خواهد شد. به \%\fBhg help config.syntax\fP\: مراجعه کنید.
.SH "فایل‌ها (FILES)"
.sp
مرکوریال در صورت وجود، داده‌های پیکربندی را از چندین پرونده می‌خواند. این پرونده‌ها به طور پیش‌فرض وجود ندارند و در صورت نیاز باید خودتان پرونده‌های پیکربندی مناسب را ایجاد کنید:
.sp
پیکربندی محلی در پرونده مختص هر مخزن به نشانی \fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\fP قرار داده می‌شود.
.sp
پیکربندی سراسری مانند تنظیم نام کاربری (username) معمولاً در مسیرهای زیر قرار می‌گیرد:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%USERPROFILE%\emercurial.ini\fP (در ویندوز)
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB$HOME/.hgrc\fP (در یونیکس و Plan9)
.UNINDENT
.sp
نام این پرونده‌ها به سیستم‌عاملی که مرکوریال روی آن نصب شده است بستگی دارد. پرونده‌های \fB*.rc\fP موجود در یک دایرکتوری به ترتیب حروف الفبا خوانده می‌شوند و پرونده‌های بعدی مقادیر قبلی را بازنویسی (override) می‌کنند. در مواردی که چند مسیر در زیر آورده شده است، تنظیمات مسیرهای اولیه بر مسیرهای بعدی اولویت دارند و آن‌ها را بازنویسی می‌کنند.
.sp
در سیستم‌های یونیکس، پرونده‌های زیر بررسی می‌شوند:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\-not\-shared\fP (مختص مخزن)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\fP (مختص مخزن)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB$HOME/.hgrc\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-$HOME/.config}/hg/hgrc\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<install\-root>/etc/mercurial/hgrc\fP (مختص محل نصب)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<install\-root>/etc/mercurial/hgrc.d/*.rc\fP (مختص محل نصب)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB/etc/mercurial/hgrc\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB/etc/mercurial/hgrc.d/*.rc\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<internal>/*.rc\fP (پیش‌فرض‌ها)
.UNINDENT
.sp
در سیستم‌های ویندوز، پرونده‌های زیر بررسی می‌شوند:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\-not\-shared\fP (مختص مخزن)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\fP (مختص مخزن)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%USERPROFILE%\e.hgrc\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%USERPROFILE%\eMercurial.ini\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%HOME%\e.hgrc\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%HOME%\eMercurial.ini\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBHKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\eSOFTWARE\eMercurial\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<install\-dir>\ehgrc.d\e*.rc\fP (مختص محل نصب)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<install\-dir>\eMercurial.ini\fP (مختص محل نصب)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%PROGRAMDATA%\eMercurial\ehgrc\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%PROGRAMDATA%\eMercurial\eMercurial.ini\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB%PROGRAMDATA%\eMercurial\ehgrc.d\e*.rc\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<internal>/*.rc\fP (پیش‌فرض‌ها)
.UNINDENT
.IP "نکته"
.
کلید رجیستری \fBHKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\eSOFTWARE\eWow6432Node\eMercurial\fP
هنگام اجرای نسخه ۳۲ بیتی پایتون در ویندوز ۶۴ بیتی مورد استفاده قرار می‌گیرد.
.RE
.sp
در سیستم‌های Plan9، پرونده‌های زیر بررسی می‌شوند:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\-not\-shared\fP (مختص مخزن)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<repo>/.hg/hgrc\fP (مختص مخزن)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB$home/lib/hgrc\fP (مختص کاربر)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<install\-root>/lib/mercurial/hgrc\fP (مختص محل نصب)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<install\-root>/lib/mercurial/hgrc.d/*.rc\fP (مختص محل نصب)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB/lib/mercurial/hgrc\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB/lib/mercurial/hgrc.d/*.rc\fP (مختص کل سیستم)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fB<internal>/*.rc\fP (پیش‌فرض‌ها)
.UNINDENT
.sp
گزینه‌های پیکربندی مختص مخزن فقط در همان مخزن خاص اعمال می‌شوند. این پرونده تحت کنترل نسخه قرار نمی‌گیرد و در طول عملیات شبیه‌سازی (clone) منتقل نخواهد شد. گزینه‌های این پرونده بر گزینه‌های سایر پرونده‌های پیکربندی اولویت دارند و آن‌ها را بازنویسی می‌کنند.
.sp
در سیستم‌های Plan 9 و یونیکس، در صورتی که این پرونده متعلق به یک کاربر مورد اعتماد یا گروه مورد اعتماد نباشد، اکثر بخش‌های آن نادیده گرفته می‌شود. برای جزئیات بیشتر به \%\fBhg help config.trusted\fP\: مراجعه کنید.
.sp
پرونده‌های پیکربندی مختص کاربر، متعلق به کاربری هستند که مرکوریال را اجرا می‌کند. گزینه‌های موجود در این پرونده‌ها روی تمام دستورات مرکوریال اجرا شده توسط این کاربر در هر دایرکتوری اعمال می‌شوند. این گزینه‌ها بر تنظیمات کل سیستم و تنظیمات محل نصب اولویت دارند.
.sp
پرونده‌های پیکربندی محل نصب در دایرکتوری محل نصب مرکوریال جستجو می‌شوند. شناسه \fB<install\-root>\fP دایرکتوری والد فایل اجرایی (یا پیوند نمادین) \fBhg\fP در حال اجرا است.
.sp
برای مثال، اگر در \fB/shared/tools/bin/hg\fP نصب شده باشد، مرکوریال مسیر \fB/shared/tools/etc/mercurial/hgrc\fP را بررسی می‌کند. گزینه‌های این پرونده‌ها بر کلیه دستورات مرکوریال اجرا شده توسط هر کاربری در هر پوشه‌ای اعمال می‌گردند.
.sp
پرونده‌های پیکربندی مختص سیستم برای کل سیستمی است که مرکوریال روی آن اجرا می‌شود. کلیدهای رجیستری حاوی رشته‌های شبیه به PATH هستند که هر بخش آن باید به یک پرونده \fBMercurial.ini\fP اشاره کند یا دایرکتوری‌ای باشد که پرونده‌های \fB*.rc\fP از آن خوانده می‌شوند. مرکوریال هر یک از این مکان‌ها را به ترتیب مشخص‌شده بررسی می‌کند تا زمانی که یک یا چند پرونده پیکربندی شناسایی شوند.
.sp
گزینه‌های پرونده‌های پیکربندی مختص سیستم روی تمام دستورات مرکوریال که توسط هر کاربری در هر مسیری اجرا شوند اعمال می‌گردند و بر گزینه‌های محل نصب اولویت دارند.
.sp
مرکوریال همراه با برخی تنظیمات پیش‌فرض ارائه می‌شود. پرونده‌های پیکربندی پیش‌فرض به همراه مرکوریال نصب شده و در هنگام ارتقاء رونویسی می‌شوند. این پرونده‌ها هرگز نباید توسط کاربران یا مدیران ویرایش شوند، اما می‌توان مقادیر آن‌ها را در سایر پرونده‌های پیکربندی بازنویسی کرد. تاکنون این پوشه تنها حاوی پیکربندی ابزار ادغام (merge) است، اما بسته‌بندها نیز می‌توانند تنظیمات پیش‌فرض دیگری را در آنجا قرار دهند.
.sp
در نسخه‌های 5.7 و جدیدتر، در صورت فعال بودن قابلیت share\-safe، اشتراک‌گذاری‌ها پرونده کانفیگ منبع اشتراک را نیز می‌خوانند.
پرونده \fI<share\-source/.hg/hgrc>\fP پیش از خواندن \fI<repo/.hg/hgrc>\fP خوانده می‌شود.
.sp
برای تنظیماتی که نباید به اشتراک گذاشته شوند، باید از \fI<repo/.hg/hgrc\-not\-shared>\fP استفاده شود.
.SH "نحو (SYNTAX)"
.sp
یک پرونده پیکربندی از بخش‌هایی تشکیل می‌شود که با یک سرآیند \fB[section]\fP شروع شده و به دنبال آن ورودی‌های \fBname = value\fP قرار می‌گیرند (که گاهی اوقات \fBکلیدهای پیکربندی\fP نامیده می‌شوند):
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[spam]
eggs=ham
green=
   eggs
.ft P
.fi
.sp
هر خط شامل یک ورودی است. اگر خطوط بعدی دارای تورفتگی (indent) باشند، به عنوان ادامه همان ورودی در نظر گرفته می‌شوند. فاصله‌های خالی ابتدای مقادیر حذف می‌شوند. خطوط خالی نادیده گرفته می‌شوند. خطوطی که با \fB#\fP یا \fB;\fP آغاز شوند نادیده گرفته شده و می‌توانند به عنوان یادداشت (کامنت) استفاده گردند.
.sp
کلیدهای پیکربندی را می‌توان چندین بار تعیین کرد؛ در این حالت مرکوریال از آخرین مقدار تنظیم‌شده استفاده خواهد کرد. به عنوان مثال:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[spam]
eggs=large
ham=serrano
eggs=small
.ft P
.fi
.sp
این کار کلید پیکربندی به نام \fBeggs\fP را روی \fBsmall\fP تنظیم می‌کند.
.sp
همچنین امکان تعریف چندباره یک بخش وجود دارد. یک بخش را می‌توان در همان پرونده یا در پرونده‌های پیکربندی مختلف بازتعریف کرد. به عنوان مثال:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[foo]
eggs=large
ham=serrano
eggs=small

[bar]
eggs=ham
green=
   eggs

[foo]
ham=prosciutto
eggs=medium
bread=toasted
.ft P
.fi
.sp
این کار کلیدهای پیکربندی \fBeggs\fP، \fBham\fP و \fBbread\fP در بخش \fBfoo\fP را به ترتیب برابر \fBmedium\fP، \fBprosciutto\fP و \fBtoasted\fP قرار می‌دهد. همان‌طور که مشاهده می‌کنید، تنها چیزی که اهمیت دارد آخرین مقدار تعیین‌شده برای هر یک از کلیدهای پیکربندی است.
.sp
اگر یک کلید پیکربندی چندین بار در پرونده‌های پیکربندی مختلف تعیین شود، مقدار نهایی به ترتیبی بستگی خواهد داشت که پرونده‌های پیکربندی مختلف خوانده می‌شوند؛ به این صورت که تنظیمات مسیرهای اولیه مسیرهای بعدی را بازنویسی می‌کنند، همان‌طور که در بخش \fBفایل‌ها\fP در بالا توضیح داده شد.
.sp
خطی به فرم \fB%include file\fP پرونده \fBfile\fP را در پرونده پیکربندی جاری وارد (include) می‌کند. این الحاق به صورت بازگشتی است؛ به این معنی که پرونده‌های واردشده می‌توانند خود پرونده‌های دیگری را وارد کنند. نام پرونده‌ها نسبت به پرونده پیکربندی که دستور \fB%include\fP در آن یافت شده است سنجیده می‌شود. متغیرهای محیطی و ساختارهای \fB~user\fP در نام پرونده گسترش می‌یابند. این به شما امکان می‌دهد کارهایی مانند زیر را انجام دهید:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
%include ~/.hgrc.d/$HOST.rc
.ft P
.fi
.sp
تا در هر رایانه‌ای که استفاده می‌کنید یک پرونده پیکربندی متفاوت وارد شود.
.sp
خطی با عبارت \fB%unset name\fP مقدار \fBname\fP را از بخش جاری حذف می‌کند، در صورتی که قبلاً تعیین شده باشد.
.sp
مقادیر یا رشته‌های متنی با قالب آزاد هستند، یا فهرستی از رشته‌های متنی، و یا مقادیر بولی. مقادیر بولی می‌توانند با استفاده از هر یک از مقادیر "1"، "yes"، "true" یا "on" به عنوان درست (true) و با "0"، "no"، "false" یا "off" به عنوان نادرست (false) تنظیم شوند (بدون حساسیت به حروف بزرگ و کوچک).
.sp
مقادیر فهرستی با فاصله یا کاما از یکدیگر جدا می‌شوند، مگر زمانی که مقادیر درون علامت نقل‌قول دوتایی (گیومه) قرار گیرند:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
allow_read = "John Doe, PhD", brian, betty
.ft P
.fi
.sp
علامت‌های نقل‌قول را می‌توان با افزودن یک بک‌اسلش پیش از آن‌ها اسکیپ کرد. فقط علامت نقل‌قول در ابتدای یک کلمه به عنوان گیومه در نظر گرفته می‌شود (برای مثال، \fBfoo"bar baz\fP فهرستی از دو عضو \fBfoo"bar\fP و \fBbaz\fP است).
.SH "بخش‌ها (SECTIONS)"
.sp
این بخش به توصیف بخش‌های مختلفی که ممکن است در یک پرونده پیکربندی مرکوریال ظاهر شوند، هدف هر بخش، کلیدهای ممکن آن و مقادیر مجاز آن‌ها می‌پردازد.
.SS \fBalias\fP
.sp
Defines command aliases.
.sp
Aliases allow you to define your own commands in terms of other
commands (or aliases), optionally including arguments. Positional
arguments in the form of \fB$1\fP, \fB$2\fP, etc. in the alias definition
are expanded by Mercurial before execution. Positional arguments not
already used by \fB$N\fP in the definition are put at the end of the
command to be executed.
.sp
Alias definitions consist of lines of the form:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
<alias> = <command> [<argument>]...
.ft P
.fi
.sp
For example, this definition:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
latest = log \-\-limit 5
.ft P
.fi
.sp
creates a new command \fBlatest\fP that shows only the five most recent
changesets. You can define subsequent aliases using earlier ones:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
stable5 = latest \-b stable
.ft P
.fi
.IP Note
.
It is possible to create aliases with the same names as
existing commands, which will then override the original
definitions. This is almost always a bad idea!
.RE
.sp
An alias can start with an exclamation point (\fB!\fP) to make it a
shell alias. A shell alias is executed with the shell and will let you
run arbitrary commands. As an example,
.sp
.nf
.ft C
echo = !echo $@
.ft P
.fi
.sp
will let you do \fBhg echo foo\fP to have \fBfoo\fP printed in your
terminal. A better example might be:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
purge = !$HG status \-\-no\-status \-\-unknown \-0 re: | xargs \-0 rm \-f
.ft P
.fi
.sp
which will make \fBhg purge\fP delete all unknown files in the
repository in the same manner as the purge extension.
.sp
Positional arguments like \fB$1\fP, \fB$2\fP, etc. in the alias definition
expand to the command arguments. Unmatched arguments are
removed. \fB$0\fP expands to the alias name and \fB$@\fP expands to all
arguments separated by a space. \fB"$@"\fP (with quotes) expands to all
arguments quoted individually and separated by a space. These expansions
happen before the command is passed to the shell.
.sp
Shell aliases are executed in an environment where \fB$HG\fP expands to
the path of the Mercurial that was used to execute the alias. This is
useful when you want to call further Mercurial commands in a shell
alias, as was done above for the purge alias. In addition,
\fB$HG_ARGS\fP expands to the arguments given to Mercurial. In the \fBhg
echo foo\fP call above, \fB$HG_ARGS\fP would expand to \fBecho foo\fP.
.IP Note
.
Some global configuration options such as \fB\-R\fP are
processed before shell aliases and will thus not be passed to
aliases.
.RE
.SS \fBannotate\fP
.sp
Settings used when displaying file annotations. All values are
Booleans and default to False. See \%\fBhg help config.diff\fP\: for
related options for the diff command.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBignorews\fP
.sp
Ignore white space when comparing lines.
.TP
.B \fBignorewseol\fP
.sp
Ignore white space at the end of a line when comparing lines.
.TP
.B \fBignorewsamount\fP
.sp
Ignore changes in the amount of white space.
.TP
.B \fBignoreblanklines\fP
.sp
Ignore changes whose lines are all blank.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBauth\fP
.sp
Authentication credentials and other authentication\-like configuration
for HTTP connections. This section allows you to store usernames and
passwords for use when logging \fIinto\fP HTTP servers. See
\%\fBhg help config.web\fP\: if you want to configure \fIwho\fP can login to
your HTTP server.
.sp
The following options apply to all hosts.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBcookiefile\fP
.sp
Path to a file containing HTTP cookie lines. Cookies matching a
host will be sent automatically.
.sp
The file format uses the Mozilla cookies.txt format, which defines cookies
on their own lines. Each line contains 7 fields delimited by the tab
character (domain, is_domain_cookie, path, is_secure, expires, name,
value). For more info, do an Internet search for "Netscape cookies.txt
format."
.sp
Note: the cookies parser does not handle port numbers on domains. You
will need to remove ports from the domain for the cookie to be recognized.
This could result in a cookie being disclosed to an unwanted server.
.sp
The cookies file is read\-only.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Other options in this section are grouped by name and have the following
format:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
<name>.<argument> = <value>
.ft P
.fi
.sp
where \fB<name>\fP is used to group arguments into authentication
entries. Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
foo.prefix = hg.intevation.de/mercurial
foo.username = foo
foo.password = bar
foo.schemes = http https

bar.prefix = secure.example.org
bar.key = path/to/file.key
bar.cert = path/to/file.cert
bar.schemes = https
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Supported arguments:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBprefix\fP
.sp
Either \fB*\fP or a URI prefix with or without the scheme part.
The authentication entry with the longest matching prefix is used
(where \fB*\fP matches everything and counts as a match of length
1). If the prefix doesn\(aqt include a scheme, the match is performed
against the URI with its scheme stripped as well, and the schemes
argument, q.v., is then subsequently consulted.
.TP
.B \fBusername\fP
.sp
Optional. Username to authenticate with. If not given, and the
remote site requires basic or digest authentication, the user will
be prompted for it. Environment variables are expanded in the
username letting you do \fBfoo.username = $USER\fP. If the URI
includes a username, only \fB[auth]\fP entries with a matching
username or without a username will be considered.
.TP
.B \fBpassword\fP
.sp
Optional. Password to authenticate with. If not given, and the
remote site requires basic or digest authentication, the user
will be prompted for it.
.TP
.B \fBkey\fP
.sp
Optional. PEM encoded client certificate key file. Environment
variables are expanded in the filename.
.TP
.B \fBcert\fP
.sp
Optional. PEM encoded client certificate chain file. Environment
variables are expanded in the filename.
.TP
.B \fBschemes\fP
.sp
Optional. Space separated list of URI schemes to use this
authentication entry with. Only used if the prefix doesn\(aqt include
a scheme. Supported schemes are http and https. They will match
static\-http and static\-https respectively, as well.
(default: https)
.UNINDENT
.sp
If no suitable authentication entry is found, the user is prompted
for credentials as usual if required by the remote.
.SS \fBcensor\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBpolicy\fP
.sp
Control how to react when accessing censored content.
Accepted value: "abort", "ignore". Defaults to abort.
.sp
A few informative commands such as \fBhg grep\fP will unconditionally ignore
censored data and merely report that it was encountered.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBcmdserver\fP
.sp
Controls command server settings. (ADVANCED)
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBmessage\-encodings\fP
.sp
List of encodings for the \fBm\fP (message) channel. The first encoding
supported by the server will be selected and advertised in the hello
message. This is useful only when \fBui.message\-output\fP is set to
\fBchannel\fP. Supported encodings are \fBcbor\fP.
.TP
.B \fBshutdown\-on\-interrupt\fP
.sp
If set to false, the server\(aqs main loop will continue running after
SIGINT received. \fBruncommand\fP requests can still be interrupted by
SIGINT. Close the write end of the pipe to shut down the server
process gracefully.
(default: True)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBcolor\fP
.sp
Configure the Mercurial color mode. For details about how to define your custom
effect and style see \%\fBhg help color\fP\:.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBmode\fP
.sp
String: control the method used to output color. One of \fBauto\fP, \fBansi\fP,
\fBwin32\fP, \fBterminfo\fP or \fBdebug\fP. In auto mode, Mercurial will
use ANSI mode by default (or win32 mode prior to Windows 10) if it detects a
terminal. Any invalid value will disable color.
.TP
.B \fBpagermode\fP
.sp
String: optional override of \fBcolor.mode\fP used with pager.
.sp
On some systems, terminfo mode may cause problems when using
color with \fBless \-R\fP as a pager program. less with the \-R option
will only display ECMA\-48 color codes, and terminfo mode may sometimes
emit codes that less doesn\(aqt understand. You can work around this by
either using ansi mode (or auto mode), or by using less \-r (which will
pass through all terminal control codes, not just color control
codes).
.sp
On some systems (such as MSYS in Windows), the terminal may support
a different color mode than the pager program.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBcommands\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBcommit.post\-status\fP
.sp
Show status of files in the working directory after successful commit.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBmerge.require\-rev\fP
.sp
Require that the revision to merge the current commit with be specified on
the command line. If this is enabled and a revision is not specified, the
command aborts.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBpush.require\-revs\fP
.sp
Require revisions to push be specified using one or more mechanisms such as
specifying them positionally on the command line, using \fB\-r\fP, \fB\-b\fP,
and/or \fB\-B\fP on the command line, or using \fBpaths.<path>:pushrev\fP in the
configuration. If this is enabled and revisions are not specified, the
command aborts.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBresolve.confirm\fP
.sp
Confirm before performing action if no filename is passed.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBresolve.explicit\-re\-merge\fP
.sp
Require uses of \fBhg resolve\fP to specify which action it should perform,
instead of re\-merging files by default.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBresolve.mark\-check\fP
.sp
Determines what level of checking \%\fBhg resolve \-\-mark\fP\: will perform before
marking files as resolved. Valid values are \fBnone\(ga, \(ga\(gawarn\fP, and
\fBabort\fP. \fBwarn\fP will output a warning listing the file(s) that still
have conflict markers in them, but will still mark everything resolved.
\fBabort\fP will output the same warning but will not mark things as resolved.
If \-\-all is passed and this is set to \fBabort\fP, only a warning will be
shown (an error will not be raised).
(default: \fBnone\fP)
.TP
.B \fBstatus.relative\fP
.sp
Make paths in \%\fBhg status\fP\: output relative to the current directory.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBstatus.terse\fP
.sp
Default value for the \-\-terse flag, which condenses status output.
(default: empty)
.TP
.B \fBupdate.check\fP
.sp
Determines what level of checking \%\fBhg update\fP\: will perform before moving
to a destination revision. Valid values are \fBabort\fP, \fBnone\fP,
\fBlinear\fP, and \fBnoconflict\fP.
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBabort\fP always fails if the working directory has uncommitted changes.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBnone\fP performs no checking, and may result in a merge with uncommitted changes.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBlinear\fP allows any update as long as it follows a straight line in the
revision history, and may trigger a merge with uncommitted changes.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBnoconflict\fP will allow any update which would not trigger a merge with
uncommitted changes, if any are present.
.UNINDENT
.sp
(default: \fBlinear\fP)
.TP
.B \fBupdate.requiredest\fP
.sp
Require that the user pass a destination when running \%\fBhg update\fP\:.
For example, \%\fBhg update .::\fP\: will be allowed, but a plain \%\fBhg update\fP\:
will be disallowed.
(default: False)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBcommittemplate\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBchangeset\fP
.sp
String: configuration in this section is used as the template to
customize the text shown in the editor when committing.
.UNINDENT
.sp
In addition to pre\-defined template keywords, commit log specific one
below can be used for customization:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBextramsg\fP
.sp
String: Extra message (typically \(aqLeave message empty to abort
commit.\(aq). This may be changed by some commands or extensions.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example, the template configuration below shows as same text as
one shown by default:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[committemplate]
changeset = {desc}\en\en
    HG: Enter commit message.  Lines beginning with \(aqHG:\(aq are removed.
    HG: {extramsg}
    HG: \-\-
    HG: user: {author}\en{ifeq(p2rev, "\-1", "",
   "HG: branch merge\en")
   }HG: branch \(aq{branch}\(aq\en{if(activebookmark,
   "HG: bookmark \(aq{activebookmark}\(aq\en")   }{subrepos %
   "HG: subrepo {subrepo}\en"              }{file_adds %
   "HG: added {file}\en"                   }{file_mods %
   "HG: changed {file}\en"                 }{file_dels %
   "HG: removed {file}\en"                 }{if(files, "",
   "HG: no files changed\en")}
.ft P
.fi
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBdiff()\fP
.sp
String: show the diff (see \%\fBhg help templates\fP\: for detail)
.UNINDENT
.sp
Sometimes it is helpful to show the diff of the changeset in the editor without
having to prefix \(aqHG: \(aq to each line so that highlighting works correctly. For
this, Mercurial provides a special string which will ignore everything below
it:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
HG: \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- >8 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
.ft P
.fi
.sp
For example, the template configuration below will show the diff below the
extra message:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[committemplate]
changeset = {desc}\en\en
    HG: Enter commit message.  Lines beginning with \(aqHG:\(aq are removed.
    HG: {extramsg}
    HG: \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- >8 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
    HG: Do not touch the line above.
    HG: Everything below will be removed.
    {diff()}
.ft P
.fi
.IP Note
.
For some problematic encodings (see \%\fBhg help win32mbcs\fP\: for
detail), this customization should be configured carefully, to
avoid showing broken characters.
.sp
For example, if a multibyte character ending with backslash (0x5c) is
followed by the ASCII character \(aqn\(aq in the customized template,
the sequence of backslash and \(aqn\(aq is treated as line\-feed unexpectedly
(and the multibyte character is broken, too).
.RE
.sp
Customized template is used for commands below (\fB\-\-edit\fP may be
required):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg backout\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg commit\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg fetch\fP\: (for merge commit only)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg graft\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg histedit\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg import\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg qfold\fP\:, \%\fBhg qnew\fP\: and \%\fBhg qrefresh\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg rebase\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg shelve\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg sign\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg tag\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\%\fBhg transplant\fP\:
.UNINDENT
.sp
Configuring items below instead of \fBchangeset\fP allows showing
customized message only for specific actions, or showing different
messages for each action.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.backout\fP for \%\fBhg backout\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.commit.amend.merge\fP for \%\fBhg commit \-\-amend\fP\: on merges
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.commit.amend.normal\fP for \%\fBhg commit \-\-amend\fP\: on other
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.commit.normal.merge\fP for \%\fBhg commit\fP\: on merges
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.commit.normal.normal\fP for \%\fBhg commit\fP\: on other
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.fetch\fP for \%\fBhg fetch\fP\: (impling merge commit)
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.gpg.sign\fP for \%\fBhg sign\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.graft\fP for \%\fBhg graft\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.histedit.edit\fP for \fBedit\fP of \%\fBhg histedit\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.histedit.fold\fP for \fBfold\fP of \%\fBhg histedit\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.histedit.mess\fP for \fBmess\fP of \%\fBhg histedit\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.histedit.pick\fP for \fBpick\fP of \%\fBhg histedit\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.import.bypass\fP for \%\fBhg import \-\-bypass\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.import.normal.merge\fP for \%\fBhg import\fP\: on merges
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.import.normal.normal\fP for \%\fBhg import\fP\: on other
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.mq.qnew\fP for \%\fBhg qnew\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.mq.qfold\fP for \%\fBhg qfold\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.mq.qrefresh\fP for \%\fBhg qrefresh\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.rebase.collapse\fP for \%\fBhg rebase \-\-collapse\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.rebase.merge\fP for \%\fBhg rebase\fP\: on merges
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.rebase.normal\fP for \%\fBhg rebase\fP\: on other
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.shelve.shelve\fP for \%\fBhg shelve\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.tag.add\fP for \%\fBhg tag\fP\: without \fB\-\-remove\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.tag.remove\fP for \%\fBhg tag \-\-remove\fP\:
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.transplant.merge\fP for \%\fBhg transplant\fP\: on merges
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBchangeset.transplant.normal\fP for \%\fBhg transplant\fP\: on other
.UNINDENT
.sp
These dot\-separated lists of names are treated as hierarchical ones.
For example, \fBchangeset.tag.remove\fP customizes the commit message
only for \%\fBhg tag \-\-remove\fP\:, but \fBchangeset.tag\fP customizes the
commit message for \%\fBhg tag\fP\: regardless of \fB\-\-remove\fP option.
.sp
When the external editor is invoked for a commit, the corresponding
dot\-separated list of names without the \fBchangeset.\fP prefix
(e.g. \fBcommit.normal.normal\fP) is in the \fBHGEDITFORM\fP environment
variable.
.sp
In this section, items other than \fBchangeset\fP can be referred from
others. For example, the configuration to list committed files up
below can be referred as \fB{listupfiles}\fP:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[committemplate]
listupfiles = {file_adds %
   "HG: added {file}\en"     }{file_mods %
   "HG: changed {file}\en"   }{file_dels %
   "HG: removed {file}\en"   }{if(files, "",
   "HG: no files changed\en")}
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBdecode/encode\fP
.sp
Filters for transforming files on checkout/checkin. This would
typically be used for newline processing or other
localization/canonicalization of files.
.sp
Filters consist of a filter pattern followed by a filter command.
Filter patterns are globs by default, rooted at the repository root.
For example, to match any file ending in \fB.txt\fP in the root
directory only, use the pattern \fB*.txt\fP. To match any file ending
in \fB.c\fP anywhere in the repository, use the pattern \fB**.c\fP.
For each file only the first matching filter applies.
.sp
The filter command can start with a specifier, either \fBpipe:\fP or
\fBtempfile:\fP. If no specifier is given, \fBpipe:\fP is used by default.
.sp
A \fBpipe:\fP command must accept data on stdin and return the transformed
data on stdout.
.sp
Pipe example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[encode]
# uncompress gzip files on checkin to improve delta compression
# note: not necessarily a good idea, just an example
*.gz = pipe: gunzip

[decode]
# recompress gzip files when writing them to the working dir (we
# can safely omit "pipe:", because it\(aqs the default)
*.gz = gzip
.ft P
.fi
.sp
A \fBtempfile:\fP command is a template. The string \fBINFILE\fP is replaced
with the name of a temporary file that contains the data to be
filtered by the command. The string \fBOUTFILE\fP is replaced with the name
of an empty temporary file, where the filtered data must be written by
the command.
.IP Note
.
The tempfile mechanism is recommended for Windows systems,
where the standard shell I/O redirection operators often have
strange effects and may corrupt the contents of your files.
.RE
.sp
This filter mechanism is used internally by the \fBeol\fP extension to
translate line ending characters between Windows (CRLF) and Unix (LF)
format. We suggest you use the \fBeol\fP extension for convenience.
.SS \fBdefaults\fP
.sp
(defaults are deprecated. Don\(aqt use them. Use aliases instead.)
.sp
Use the \fB[defaults]\fP section to define command defaults, i.e. the
default options/arguments to pass to the specified commands.
.sp
The following example makes \%\fBhg log\fP\: run in verbose mode, and
\%\fBhg status\fP\: show only the modified files, by default:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[defaults]
log = \-v
status = \-m
.ft P
.fi
.sp
The actual commands, instead of their aliases, must be used when
defining command defaults. The command defaults will also be applied
to the aliases of the commands defined.
.SS \fBdiff\fP
.sp
Settings used when displaying diffs. Everything except for \fBunified\fP
is a Boolean and defaults to False. See \%\fBhg help config.annotate\fP\:
for related options for the annotate command.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBgit\fP
.sp
Use git extended diff format.
.TP
.B \fBmerge\fP
.sp
Show a "merge diff" instead of defaulting to a diff against
the working directory\(aqs first parent. A "merge diff" will diff against the
conflict regions in the merge, which is very useful to make sense of what
the merge commit itself has done.
.TP
.B \fBnobinary\fP
.sp
Omit git binary patches.
.TP
.B \fBnodates\fP
.sp
Don\(aqt include dates in diff headers.
.TP
.B \fBnoprefix\fP
.sp
Omit \(aqa/\(aq and \(aqb/\(aq prefixes from filenames. Ignored in plain mode.
.TP
.B \fBshowfunc\fP
.sp
Show which function each change is in.
.TP
.B \fBignorews\fP
.sp
Ignore white space when comparing lines.
.TP
.B \fBignorewsamount\fP
.sp
Ignore changes in the amount of white space.
.TP
.B \fBignoreblanklines\fP
.sp
Ignore changes whose lines are all blank.
.TP
.B \fBunified\fP
.sp
Number of lines of context to show.
.TP
.B \fBword\-diff\fP
.sp
Highlight changed words.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBemail\fP
.sp
Settings for extensions that send email messages.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBfrom\fP
.sp
Optional. Email address to use in "From" header and SMTP envelope
of outgoing messages.
.TP
.B \fBto\fP
.sp
Optional. Comma\-separated list of recipients\(aq email addresses.
.TP
.B \fBcc\fP
.sp
Optional. Comma\-separated list of carbon copy recipients\(aq
email addresses.
.TP
.B \fBbcc\fP
.sp
Optional. Comma\-separated list of blind carbon copy recipients\(aq
email addresses.
.TP
.B \fBmethod\fP
.sp
Optional. Method to use to send email messages. If value is \fBsmtp\fP
(default), use SMTP (see the \fB[smtp]\fP section for configuration).
Otherwise, use as name of program to run that acts like sendmail
(takes \fB\-f\fP option for sender, list of recipients on command line,
message on stdin). Normally, setting this to \fBsendmail\fP or
\fB/usr/sbin/sendmail\fP is enough to use sendmail to send messages.
.TP
.B \fBcharsets\fP
.sp
Optional. Comma\-separated list of character sets considered
convenient for recipients. Addresses, headers, and parts not
containing patches of outgoing messages will be encoded in the
first character set to which conversion from local encoding
(\fB$HGENCODING\fP, \fBui.fallbackencoding\fP) succeeds. If correct
conversion fails, the text in question is sent as is.
(default: \(aq\(aq)
.sp
Order of outgoing email character sets:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP 1. 3
.
\fBus\-ascii\fP: always first, regardless of settings
.IP 2. 3
.
\fBemail.charsets\fP: in order given by user
.IP 3. 3
.
\fBui.fallbackencoding\fP: if not in email.charsets
.IP 4. 3
.
\fB$HGENCODING\fP: if not in email.charsets
.IP 5. 3
.
\fButf\-8\fP: always last, regardless of settings
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Email example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[email]
from = Joseph User <joe.user@example.com>
method = /usr/sbin/sendmail
# charsets for western Europeans
# us\-ascii, utf\-8 omitted, as they are tried first and last
charsets = iso\-8859\-1, iso\-8859\-15, windows\-1252
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBextensions\fP
.sp
Mercurial has an extension mechanism for adding new features. To
enable an extension, create an entry for it in this section.
.sp
If you know that the extension is already in Python\(aqs search path,
you can give the name of the module, followed by \fB=\fP, with nothing
after the \fB=\fP.
.sp
Otherwise, give a name that you choose, followed by \fB=\fP, followed by
the path to the \fB.py\fP file (including the file name extension) that
defines the extension.
.sp
To explicitly disable an extension that is enabled in an hgrc of
broader scope, prepend its path with \fB!\fP, as in \fBfoo = !/ext/path\fP
or \fBfoo = !\fP when path is not supplied.
.sp
Example for \fB~/.hgrc\fP:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[extensions]
# (the churn extension will get loaded from Mercurial\(aqs path)
churn =
# (this extension will get loaded from the file specified)
myfeature = ~/.hgext/myfeature.py
.ft P
.fi
.sp
If an extension fails to load, a warning will be issued, and Mercurial will
proceed. To enforce that an extension must be loaded, one can set the \fIrequired\fP
suboption in the config:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[extensions]
myfeature = ~/.hgext/myfeature.py
myfeature:required = yes
.ft P
.fi
.sp
To debug extension loading issue, one can add \fI\-\-traceback\fP to their mercurial
invocation.
.sp
A default setting can we set using the special \fI*\fP extension key:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[extensions]
*:required = yes
myfeature = ~/.hgext/myfeature.py
rebase=
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBformat\fP
.sp
Configuration that controls the repository format. Newer format options are more
powerful, but incompatible with some older versions of Mercurial.
.sp
Format options are considered at repository initialization only. You need to
make a new clone for config changes to be taken into account.
.sp
For more details about repository format and version compatibility, see
\%https://www.mercurial\-scm.org/wiki/MissingRequirement\:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBusegeneraldelta\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the "generaldelta" repository format which improves
repository compression by allowing "revlog" to store deltas against
arbitrary revisions instead of the previously stored one. This provides
significant improvement for repositories with branches.
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial version 1.9.
.sp
Enabled by default. Implied by \fIsparse\-revlog\fP
.TP
.B \fBdotencode\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the "dotencode" repository format which enhances
the "fncache" repository format (which has to be enabled to use
dotencode) to avoid issues with filenames starting with "._" on
Mac OS X and spaces on Windows.
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial version 1.7.
.sp
Enabled by default.
.TP
.B \fBusefncache\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the "fncache" repository format which enhances
the "store" repository format (which has to be enabled to use
fncache) to allow longer filenames and avoids using Windows
reserved names, e.g. "nul".
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial version 1.1.
.sp
Enabled by default.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-delta\-info\-flags\fP
.sp
Store more information through flags in the revlog\(aqs index.
.sp
These flags allow for more efficient data access, data exchange and
data storage in general.
.sp
In particular, one can expect better performance for the following categories of
commands for repositories using this format can expect better performance than
ones using older formats:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg status \-\-copies \-\-rev X\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg diff\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg merge\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg clone\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg pull\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg push\fP
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIhg unbundle\fP
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBuse\-dirstate\-v2\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the experimental "dirstate\-v2" feature. The dirstate
functionality is shared by all commands interacting with the working copy.
The new version is more robust, faster and stores more information.
.sp
The performance\-improving version of this feature is currently only
implemented in Rust (see \%\fBhg help rust\fP\:), so people not using a version of
Mercurial compiled with the Rust parts might actually suffer some slowdown.
For this reason, such versions will by default refuse to access repositories
with "dirstate\-v2" enabled.
.sp
This behavior can be adjusted via configuration: check
\%\fBhg help config.storage.dirstate\-v2.slow\-path\fP\: for details.
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial 6.0 or above.
.sp
By default this format variant is disabled if the fast implementation is not
available, and enabled by default if the fast implementation is available.
.sp
To accomodate installations of Mercurial without the fast implementation,
you can downgrade your repository. To do so run the following command:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ hg debugupgraderepo \e
      \-\-run \e
      \-\-config format.use\-dirstate\-v2=False \e
      \-\-config storage.dirstate\-v2.slow\-path=allow
.ft P
.fi
.sp
For a more comprehensive guide, see \%\fBhg help internals.dirstate\-v2\fP\:.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-dirstate\-v2.automatic\-upgrade\-of\-mismatching\-repositories\fP
.sp
When enabled, an automatic upgrade will be triggered when a repository format
does not match its \fIuse\-dirstate\-v2\fP config.
.sp
This is an advanced behavior that most users will not need. We recommend you
don\(aqt use this unless you are a seasoned administrator of a Mercurial install
base.
.sp
Automatic upgrade means that any process accessing the repository will
upgrade the repository format to use \fIdirstate\-v2\fP. This only triggers if a
change is needed. This also applies to operations that would have been
read\-only (like hg status).
.sp
If the repository cannot be locked, the automatic\-upgrade operation will be
skipped. The next operation will attempt it again.
.sp
This configuration will apply for moves in any direction, either adding the
\fIdirstate\-v2\fP format if \fIformat.use\-dirstate\-v2=yes\fP or removing the
\fIdirstate\-v2\fP requirement if \fIformat.use\-dirstate\-v2=no\fP. So we recommend
setting both this value and \fIformat.use\-dirstate\-v2\fP at the same time.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-dirstate\-v2.automatic\-upgrade\-of\-mismatching\-repositories:quiet\fP
.sp
Hide message when performing such automatic upgrade.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the writing of "tracked key" file alongside
the dirstate. (default to disabled)
.sp
That "tracked\-hint" can help external automations to detect changes to
the set of tracked files. (i.e the result of \(aqhg files\(aq or \(aqhg status
\-macd\(aq)
.sp
The tracked\-hint is written in a new \(aq.hg/dirstate\-tracked\-hint\(aq. That
file contains two lines: \- the first line is the file version
(currently: 1), \- the second line contains the "tracked\-hint". That
file is written right after the dirstate is written.
.sp
The tracked\-hint changes whenever the set of file tracked in the
dirstate changes. The general idea is: \- if the hint is identical, the
set of tracked file SHOULD be identical, \- if the hint is different,
the set of tracked file MIGHT be different.
.sp
The "hint is identical" case uses \(aqSHOULD\(aq as the dirstate and the
hint file are two distinct files and therefore that cannot be read or
written to in an atomic way. If the key is identical, nothing
garantees that the dirstate is not updated right after the hint file.
This is considered a negligible limitation for the intended usecase.
It is actually possible to prevent this race by taking the repository
lock during read operations.
.sp
They are two "ways" to use this feature:
.sp
1) monitoring changes to the \(aq.hg/dirstate\-tracked\-hint\(aq, if the file
changes, the tracked set might have changed.
.INDENT 7.0
.IP 2. 3
.
storing the value and comparing it to a later value.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBuse\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint.automatic\-upgrade\-of\-mismatching\-repositories\fP
.sp
When enabled, an automatic upgrade will be triggered when a repository format
does not match its \fIuse\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint\fP config.
.sp
This is an advanced behavior that most users will not need. We recommend you
don\(aqt use this unless you are a seasoned administrator of a Mercurial install
base.
.sp
Automatic upgrade means that any process accessing the repository will
upgrade the repository format to use \fIdirstate\-tracked\-hint\fP. This only
triggers if a change is needed. This also applies to operations that would
have been read\-only (like hg status).
.sp
If the repository cannot be locked, the automatic\-upgrade operation will be
skipped. The next operation will attempt it again.
.sp
This configuration will apply for moves in any direction, either adding the
\fIdirstate\-tracked\-hint\fP format if \fIformat.use\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint=yes\fP or
removing the \fIdirstate\-tracked\-hint\fP requirement if
\fIformat.use\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint=no\fP. So we recommend setting both this
value and \fIformat.use\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint\fP at the same time.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-dirstate\-tracked\-hint.automatic\-upgrade\-of\-mismatching\-repositories:quiet\fP
.sp
Hide message when performing such automatic upgrade.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-persistent\-nodemap\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the "persistent\-nodemap" feature which improves
performance if the Rust extensions are available.
.sp
The "persistent\-nodemap" persist the "node \-> rev" on disk removing the
need to dynamically build that mapping for each Mercurial invocation. This
significantly reduces the startup cost of various local and server\-side
operation for larger repositories.
.sp
The performance\-improving version of this feature is currently only
implemented in Rust (see \%\fBhg help rust\fP\:), so people not using a version of
Mercurial compiled with the Rust parts might actually suffer some slowdown.
For this reason, such versions will by default refuse to access repositories
with "persistent\-nodemap".
.sp
This behavior can be adjusted via configuration: check
\%\fBhg help config.storage.revlog.persistent\-nodemap.slow\-path\fP\: for details.
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial 5.4 or above.
.sp
By default this format variant is disabled if the fast implementation is not
available, and enabled by default if the fast implementation is available.
.sp
To accomodate installations of Mercurial without the fast implementation,
you can downgrade your repository. To do so run the following command:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ hg debugupgraderepo \e
      \-\-run \e
      \-\-config format.use\-persistent\-nodemap=False \e
      \-\-config storage.revlog.persistent\-nodemap.slow\-path=allow
.ft P
.fi
.TP
.B \fBuse\-share\-safe\fP
.sp
Enforce "safe" behaviors for all "shares" that access this repository.
.sp
With this feature, "shares" using this repository as a source will:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
read the source repository\(aqs configuration (\fI<source>/.hg/hgrc\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
.
read and use the source repository\(aqs "requirements"
(except the working copy specific one).
.UNINDENT
.sp
Without this feature, "shares" using this repository as a source will:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
keep tracking the repository "requirements" in the share only, ignoring
the source "requirements", possibly diverging from them.
.IP \(bu 2
.
ignore source repository config. This can create problems, like silently
ignoring important hooks.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Beware that existing shares will not be upgraded/downgraded, and by
default, Mercurial will refuse to interact with them until the mismatch
is resolved. See \%\fBhg help config.share.safe\-mismatch.source\-safe\fP\: and
\%\fBhg help config.share.safe\-mismatch.source\-not\-safe\fP\: for details.
.sp
Introduced in Mercurial 5.7.
.sp
Enabled by default in Mercurial 6.1.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-share\-safe.automatic\-upgrade\-of\-mismatching\-repositories\fP
.sp
When enabled, an automatic upgrade will be triggered when a repository format
does not match its \fIuse\-share\-safe\fP config.
.sp
This is an advanced behavior that most users will not need. We recommend you
don\(aqt use this unless you are a seasoned administrator of a Mercurial install
base.
.sp
Automatic upgrade means that any process accessing the repository will
upgrade the repository format to use \fIshare\-safe\fP. This only triggers if a
change is needed. This also applies to operation that would have been
read\-only (like hg status).
.sp
If the repository cannot be locked, the automatic\-upgrade operation will be
skipped. The next operation will attempt it again.
.sp
This configuration will apply for moves in any direction, either adding the
\fIshare\-safe\fP format if \fIformat.use\-share\-safe=yes\fP or removing the
\fIshare\-safe\fP requirement if \fIformat.use\-share\-safe=no\fP. So we recommend
setting both this value and \fIformat.use\-share\-safe\fP at the same time.
.TP
.B \fBuse\-share\-safe.automatic\-upgrade\-of\-mismatching\-repositories:quiet\fP
.sp
Hide message when performing such automatic upgrade.
.TP
.B \fBusestore\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the "store" repository format which improves
compatibility with systems that fold case or otherwise mangle
filenames. Disabling this option will allow you to store longer filenames
in some situations at the expense of compatibility.
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial version 0.9.4.
.sp
Enabled by default.
.TP
.B \fBsparse\-revlog\fP
.sp
Enable or disable the \fBsparse\-revlog\fP delta strategy. This format improves
delta re\-use inside revlog. For very branchy repositories, it results in a
smaller store. For repositories with many revisions, it also helps
performance (by using shortened delta chains.)
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial version 4.7
.sp
Implies \fIusegeneraldelta\fP.
.sp
Enabled by default.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog\-compression\fP
.sp
Compression algorithm used by revlog. Supported values are \fIzlib\fP and
\fIzstd\fP. The \fIzlib\fP engine is the historical default of Mercurial. \fIzstd\fP is
a newer format that is usually a net win over \fIzlib\fP, operating faster at
better compression rates. Use \fIzstd\fP to reduce CPU usage. Multiple values
can be specified, the first available one will be used.
.sp
On some systems, the Mercurial installation may lack \fIzstd\fP support.
.sp
Default is \fIzstd\fP if available, \fIzlib\fP otherwise.
.TP
.B \fBbookmarks\-in\-store\fP
.sp
Store bookmarks in .hg/store/. This means that bookmarks are shared when
using \fIhg share\fP regardless of the \fI\-B\fP option.
.sp
Repositories with this on\-disk format require Mercurial version 5.1.
.sp
Disabled by default.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBgraph\fP
.sp
Web graph view configuration. This section let you change graph
elements display properties by branches, for instance to make the
\fBdefault\fP branch stand out.
.sp
Each line has the following format:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
<branch>.<argument> = <value>
.ft P
.fi
.sp
where \fB<branch>\fP is the name of the branch being
customized. Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[graph]
# 2px width
default.width = 2
# red color
default.color = FF0000
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Supported arguments:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBwidth\fP
.sp
Set branch edges width in pixels.
.TP
.B \fBcolor\fP
.sp
Set branch edges color in hexadecimal RGB notation.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBhooks\fP
.sp
Commands or Python functions that get automatically executed by
various actions such as starting or finishing a commit. Multiple
hooks can be run for the same action by appending a suffix to the
action. Overriding a site\-wide hook can be done by changing its
value or setting it to an empty string.  Hooks can be prioritized
by adding a prefix of \fBpriority.\fP to the hook name on a new line
and setting the priority. The default priority is 0.
.sp
Example \fB.hg/hgrc\fP:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[hooks]
# update working directory after adding changesets
changegroup.update = hg update
# do not use the site\-wide hook
incoming =
incoming.email = /my/email/hook
incoming.autobuild = /my/build/hook
# force autobuild hook to run before other incoming hooks
priority.incoming.autobuild = 1
###  control HGPLAIN setting when running autobuild hook
# HGPLAIN always set (default from Mercurial 5.7)
incoming.autobuild:run\-with\-plain = yes
# HGPLAIN never set
incoming.autobuild:run\-with\-plain = no
# HGPLAIN inherited from environment (default before Mercurial 5.7)
incoming.autobuild:run\-with\-plain = auto
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Most hooks are run with environment variables set that give useful
additional information. For each hook below, the environment variables
it is passed are listed with names in the form \fB$HG_foo\fP. The
\fB$HG_HOOKTYPE\fP and \fB$HG_HOOKNAME\fP variables are set for all hooks.
They contain the type of hook which triggered the run and the full name
of the hook in the config, respectively. In the example above, this will
be \fB$HG_HOOKTYPE=incoming\fP and \fB$HG_HOOKNAME=incoming.email\fP.
.sp
Some basic Unix syntax can be enabled for portability, including \fB$VAR\fP
and \fB${VAR}\fP style variables.  A \fB~\fP followed by \fB\e\fP or \fB/\fP will
be expanded to \fB%USERPROFILE%\fP to simulate a subset of tilde expansion
on Unix.  To use a literal \fB$\fP or \fB~\fP, it must be escaped with a back
slash or inside of a strong quote.  Strong quotes will be replaced by
double quotes after processing.
.sp
This feature is enabled by adding a prefix of \fBtonative.\fP to the hook
name on a new line, and setting it to \fBTrue\fP.  For example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[hooks]
incoming.autobuild = /my/build/hook
# enable translation to cmd.exe syntax for autobuild hook
tonative.incoming.autobuild = True
.ft P
.fi
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBchangegroup\fP
.sp
Run after a changegroup has been added via push, pull or unbundle.  The ID of
the first new changeset is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP and last is in \fB$HG_NODE_LAST\fP.
The URL from which changes came is in \fB$HG_URL\fP.
.TP
.B \fBcommit\fP
.sp
Run after a changeset has been created in the local repository. The ID
of the newly created changeset is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP. Parent changeset
IDs are in \fB$HG_PARENT1\fP and \fB$HG_PARENT2\fP.
.TP
.B \fBincoming\fP
.sp
Run after a changeset has been pulled, pushed, or unbundled into
the local repository. The ID of the newly arrived changeset is in
\fB$HG_NODE\fP. The URL that was source of the changes is in \fB$HG_URL\fP.
.TP
.B \fBoutgoing\fP
.sp
Run after sending changes from the local repository to another. The ID of
first changeset sent is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP. The source of operation is in
\fB$HG_SOURCE\fP. Also see \%\fBhg help config.hooks.preoutgoing\fP\:.
.TP
.B \fBpost\-<command>\fP
.sp
Run after successful invocations of the associated command. The
contents of the command line are passed as \fB$HG_ARGS\fP and the result
code in \fB$HG_RESULT\fP. Parsed command line arguments are passed as
\fB$HG_PATS\fP and \fB$HG_OPTS\fP. These contain string representations of
the python data internally passed to <command>. \fB$HG_OPTS\fP is a
dictionary of options (with unspecified options set to their defaults).
\fB$HG_PATS\fP is a list of arguments. Hook failure is ignored.
.TP
.B \fBfail\-<command>\fP
.sp
Run after a failed invocation of an associated command. The contents
of the command line are passed as \fB$HG_ARGS\fP. Parsed command line
arguments are passed as \fB$HG_PATS\fP and \fB$HG_OPTS\fP. These contain
string representations of the python data internally passed to
<command>. \fB$HG_OPTS\fP is a dictionary of options (with unspecified
options set to their defaults). \fB$HG_PATS\fP is a list of arguments.
Hook failure is ignored.
.TP
.B \fBpre\-<command>\fP
.sp
Run before executing the associated command. The contents of the
command line are passed as \fB$HG_ARGS\fP. Parsed command line arguments
are passed as \fB$HG_PATS\fP and \fB$HG_OPTS\fP. These contain string
representations of the data internally passed to <command>. \fB$HG_OPTS\fP
is a dictionary of options (with unspecified options set to their
defaults). \fB$HG_PATS\fP is a list of arguments. If the hook returns
failure, the command doesn\(aqt execute and Mercurial returns the failure
code.
.TP
.B \fBprechangegroup\fP
.sp
Run before a changegroup is added via push, pull or unbundle. Exit
status 0 allows the changegroup to proceed. A non\-zero status will
cause the push, pull or unbundle to fail. The URL from which changes
will come is in \fB$HG_URL\fP.
.TP
.B \fBprecommit\fP
.sp
Run before starting a local commit. Exit status 0 allows the
commit to proceed. A non\-zero status will cause the commit to fail.
Parent changeset IDs are in \fB$HG_PARENT1\fP and \fB$HG_PARENT2\fP.
.TP
.B \fBprelistkeys\fP
.sp
Run before listing pushkeys (like bookmarks) in the
repository. A non\-zero status will cause failure. The key namespace is
in \fB$HG_NAMESPACE\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpreoutgoing\fP
.sp
Run before collecting changes to send from the local repository to
another. A non\-zero status will cause failure. This lets you prevent
pull over HTTP or SSH. It can also prevent propagating commits (via
local pull, push (outbound) or bundle commands), but not completely,
since you can just copy files instead. The source of operation is in
\fB$HG_SOURCE\fP. If "serve", the operation is happening on behalf of a remote
SSH or HTTP repository. If "push", "pull" or "bundle", the operation
is happening on behalf of a repository on same system.
.TP
.B \fBprepushkey\fP
.sp
Run before a pushkey (like a bookmark) is added to the
repository. A non\-zero status will cause the key to be rejected. The
key namespace is in \fB$HG_NAMESPACE\fP, the key is in \fB$HG_KEY\fP,
the old value (if any) is in \fB$HG_OLD\fP, and the new value is in
\fB$HG_NEW\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpretag\fP
.sp
Run before creating a tag. Exit status 0 allows the tag to be
created. A non\-zero status will cause the tag to fail. The ID of the
changeset to tag is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP. The name of tag is in \fB$HG_TAG\fP. The
tag is local if \fB$HG_LOCAL=1\fP, or in the repository if \fB$HG_LOCAL=0\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpretransmit\-inline\-clone\-bundle\fP
.sp
Run before transferring an inline clonebundle to the peer.
If the exit status is 0, the inline clonebundle will be allowed to be
transferred. A non\-zero status will cause the transfer to fail.
The path of the inline clonebundle is in \fB$HG_CLONEBUNDLEPATH\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpretxnopen\fP
.sp
Run before any new repository transaction is open. The reason for the
transaction will be in \fB$HG_TXNNAME\fP, and a unique identifier for the
transaction will be in \fB$HG_TXNID\fP. A non\-zero status will prevent the
transaction from being opened.
.TP
.B \fBpretxnclose\fP
.sp
Run right before the transaction is actually finalized. Any repository change
will be visible to the hook program. This lets you validate the transaction
content or change it. Exit status 0 allows the commit to proceed. A non\-zero
status will cause the transaction to be rolled back. The reason for the
transaction opening will be in \fB$HG_TXNNAME\fP, and a unique identifier for
the transaction will be in \fB$HG_TXNID\fP. The rest of the available data will
vary according the transaction type.  Changes unbundled to the repository will
add \fB$HG_URL\fP and \fB$HG_SOURCE\fP.  New changesets will add \fB$HG_NODE\fP (the
ID of the first added changeset), \fB$HG_NODE_LAST\fP (the ID of the last added
changeset).  Bookmark and phase changes will set \fB$HG_BOOKMARK_MOVED\fP and
\fB$HG_PHASES_MOVED\fP to \fB1\fP respectively.  The number of new obsmarkers, if
any, will be in \fB$HG_NEW_OBSMARKERS\fP, etc.
.TP
.B \fBpretxnclose\-bookmark\fP
.sp
Run right before a bookmark change is actually finalized. Any repository
change will be visible to the hook program. This lets you validate the
transaction content or change it. Exit status 0 allows the commit to
proceed. A non\-zero status will cause the transaction to be rolled back.
The name of the bookmark will be available in \fB$HG_BOOKMARK\fP, the new
bookmark location will be available in \fB$HG_NODE\fP while the previous
location will be available in \fB$HG_OLDNODE\fP. In case of a bookmark
creation \fB$HG_OLDNODE\fP will be empty. In case of deletion \fB$HG_NODE\fP
will be empty.
In addition, the reason for the transaction opening will be in
\fB$HG_TXNNAME\fP, and a unique identifier for the transaction will be in
\fB$HG_TXNID\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpretxnclose\-phase\fP
.sp
Run right before a phase change is actually finalized. Any repository change
will be visible to the hook program. This lets you validate the transaction
content or change it. Exit status 0 allows the commit to proceed.  A non\-zero
status will cause the transaction to be rolled back. The hook is called
multiple times, once for each revision affected by a phase change.
The affected node is available in \fB$HG_NODE\fP, the phase in \fB$HG_PHASE\fP
while the previous \fB$HG_OLDPHASE\fP. In case of new node, \fB$HG_OLDPHASE\fP
will be empty.  In addition, the reason for the transaction opening will be in
\fB$HG_TXNNAME\fP, and a unique identifier for the transaction will be in
\fB$HG_TXNID\fP. The hook is also run for newly added revisions. In this case
the \fB$HG_OLDPHASE\fP entry will be empty.
.TP
.B \fBtxnclose\fP
.sp
Run after any repository transaction has been committed. At this
point, the transaction can no longer be rolled back. The hook will run
after the lock is released. See \%\fBhg help config.hooks.pretxnclose\fP\: for
details about available variables.
.TP
.B \fBtxnclose\-bookmark\fP
.sp
Run after any bookmark change has been committed. At this point, the
transaction can no longer be rolled back. The hook will run after the lock
is released. See \%\fBhg help config.hooks.pretxnclose\-bookmark\fP\: for details
about available variables.
.TP
.B \fBtxnclose\-phase\fP
.sp
Run after any phase change has been committed. At this point, the
transaction can no longer be rolled back. The hook will run after the lock
is released. See \%\fBhg help config.hooks.pretxnclose\-phase\fP\: for details about
available variables.
.TP
.B \fBtxnabort\fP
.sp
Run when a transaction is aborted. See \%\fBhg help config.hooks.pretxnclose\fP\:
for details about available variables.
.TP
.B \fBpretxnchangegroup\fP
.sp
Run after a changegroup has been added via push, pull or unbundle, but before
the transaction has been committed. The changegroup is visible to the hook
program. This allows validation of incoming changes before accepting them.
The ID of the first new changeset is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP and last is in
\fB$HG_NODE_LAST\fP. Exit status 0 allows the transaction to commit. A non\-zero
status will cause the transaction to be rolled back, and the push, pull or
unbundle will fail. The URL that was the source of changes is in \fB$HG_URL\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpretxncommit\fP
.sp
Run after a changeset has been created, but before the transaction is
committed. The changeset is visible to the hook program. This allows
validation of the commit message and changes. Exit status 0 allows the
commit to proceed. A non\-zero status will cause the transaction to
be rolled back. The ID of the new changeset is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP. The parent
changeset IDs are in \fB$HG_PARENT1\fP and \fB$HG_PARENT2\fP.
.TP
.B \fBpreupdate\fP
.sp
Run before updating the working directory. Exit status 0 allows
the update to proceed. A non\-zero status will prevent the update.
The changeset ID of first new parent is in \fB$HG_PARENT1\fP. If updating to a
merge, the ID of second new parent is in \fB$HG_PARENT2\fP.
.TP
.B \fBlistkeys\fP
.sp
Run after listing pushkeys (like bookmarks) in the repository. The
key namespace is in \fB$HG_NAMESPACE\fP. \fB$HG_VALUES\fP is a
dictionary containing the keys and values.
.TP
.B \fBpushkey\fP
.sp
Run after a pushkey (like a bookmark) is added to the
repository. The key namespace is in \fB$HG_NAMESPACE\fP, the key is in
\fB$HG_KEY\fP, the old value (if any) is in \fB$HG_OLD\fP, and the new
value is in \fB$HG_NEW\fP.
.TP
.B \fBtag\fP
.sp
Run after a tag is created. The ID of the tagged changeset is in \fB$HG_NODE\fP.
The name of tag is in \fB$HG_TAG\fP. The tag is local if \fB$HG_LOCAL=1\fP, or in
the repository if \fB$HG_LOCAL=0\fP.
.TP
.B \fBupdate\fP
.sp
Run after updating the working directory. The changeset ID of first
new parent is in \fB$HG_PARENT1\fP. If updating to a merge, the ID of second new
parent is in \fB$HG_PARENT2\fP. If the update succeeded, \fB$HG_ERROR=0\fP. If the
update failed (e.g. because conflicts were not resolved), \fB$HG_ERROR=1\fP.
.TP
.B \fBprelock\fP
.sp
run before the store lock is taken, mostly used for test and debug.
.TP
.B \fBprewlock\fP
.sp
run before the working copy lock is taken, mostly used for test and debug.
.UNINDENT
.IP Note
.
It is generally better to use standard hooks rather than the
generic pre\- and post\- command hooks, as they are guaranteed to be
called in the appropriate contexts for influencing transactions.
Also, hooks like "commit" will be called in all contexts that
generate a commit (e.g. tag) and not just the commit command.
.RE
.IP Note
.
Environment variables with empty values may not be passed to
hooks on platforms such as Windows. As an example, \fB$HG_PARENT2\fP
will have an empty value under Unix\-like platforms for non\-merge
changesets, while it will not be available at all under Windows.
.RE
.sp
The syntax for Python hooks is as follows:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
hookname = python:modulename.submodule.callable
hookname = python:/path/to/python/module.py:callable
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Python hooks are run within the Mercurial process. Each hook is
called with at least three keyword arguments: a ui object (keyword
\fBui\fP), a repository object (keyword \fBrepo\fP), and a \fBhooktype\fP
keyword that tells what kind of hook is used. Arguments listed as
environment variables above are passed as keyword arguments, with no
\fBHG_\fP prefix, and names in lower case.
.sp
If a Python hook returns a "true" value or raises an exception, this
is treated as a failure.
.SS \fBhostfingerprints\fP
.sp
(Deprecated. Use \fB[hostsecurity]\fP\(aqs \fBfingerprints\fP options instead.)
.sp
Fingerprints of the certificates of known HTTPS servers.
.sp
A HTTPS connection to a server with a fingerprint configured here will
only succeed if the servers certificate matches the fingerprint.
This is very similar to how ssh known hosts works.
.sp
The fingerprint is the SHA\-1 hash value of the DER encoded certificate.
Multiple values can be specified (separated by spaces or commas). This can
be used to define both old and new fingerprints while a host transitions
to a new certificate.
.sp
The CA chain and web.cacerts is not used for servers with a fingerprint.
.sp
For example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[hostfingerprints]
hg.intevation.de = fc:e2:8d:d9:51:cd:cb:c1:4d:18:6b:b7:44:8d:49:72:57:e6:cd:33
hg.intevation.org = fc:e2:8d:d9:51:cd:cb:c1:4d:18:6b:b7:44:8d:49:72:57:e6:cd:33
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBhostsecurity\fP
.sp
Used to specify global and per\-host security settings for connecting to
other machines.
.sp
The following options control default behavior for all hosts.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBciphers\fP
.sp
Defines the cryptographic ciphers to use for connections.
.sp
Value must be a valid OpenSSL Cipher List Format as documented at
\%https://www.openssl.org/docs/manmaster/apps/ciphers.html#CIPHER\-LIST\-FORMAT\:.
.sp
This setting is for advanced users only. Setting to incorrect values
can significantly lower connection security or decrease performance.
You have been warned.
.sp
This option requires Python 2.7.
.TP
.B \fBminimumprotocol\fP
.sp
Defines the minimum channel encryption protocol to use in the client.
.sp
By default, the highest version of TLS supported by both client and server
is used.
.sp
Allowed values are: \fBtls1.0\fP, \fBtls1.1\fP, \fBtls1.2\fP, \fBtls1.3\fP.
.sp
Depending on the version of Python being used, not all of these values may
be available.  See \fBhg debuginstall\fP for the values supported by the
current installation.
.sp
When running a Python that supports modern TLS versions, the default is
\fBtls1.2\fP. \fBtls1.0\fP and \fBtls1.1\fP can still be used to allow TLS 1.0
or TLS 1.1 respectively, if supported by Python. However, this weakens
security and should only be used as a feature of last resort if a server
does not support TLS 1.2+.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Options in the \fB[hostsecurity]\fP section can have the form
\fBhostname\fP:\fBsetting\fP. This allows multiple settings to be defined on a
per\-host basis.
.sp
The following per\-host settings can be defined.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBciphers\fP
.sp
This behaves like \fBciphers\fP as described above except it only applies
to the host on which it is defined.
.TP
.B \fBfingerprints\fP
.sp
A list of hashes of the DER encoded peer/remote certificate. Values have
the form \fBalgorithm\fP:\fBfingerprint\fP. e.g.
\fBsha256:c3ab8ff13720e8ad9047dd39466b3c8974e592c2fa383d4a3960714caef0c4f2\fP.
In addition, colons (\fB:\fP) can appear in the fingerprint part.
.sp
The following algorithms/prefixes are supported: \fBsha1\fP, \fBsha256\fP,
\fBsha512\fP.
.sp
Use of \fBsha256\fP or \fBsha512\fP is preferred.
.sp
If a fingerprint is specified, the CA chain is not validated for this
host and Mercurial will require the remote certificate to match one
of the fingerprints specified. This means if the server updates its
certificate, Mercurial will abort until a new fingerprint is defined.
This can provide stronger security than traditional CA\-based validation
at the expense of convenience.
.sp
This option takes precedence over \fBverifycertsfile\fP.
.TP
.B \fBminimumprotocol\fP
.sp
This behaves like \fBminimumprotocol\fP as described above except it
only applies to the host on which it is defined.
.TP
.B \fBverifycertsfile\fP
.sp
Path to file a containing a list of PEM encoded certificates used to
verify the server certificate. Environment variables and \fB~user\fP
constructs are expanded in the filename.
.sp
The server certificate or the certificate\(aqs certificate authority (CA)
must match a certificate from this file or certificate verification
will fail and connections to the server will be refused.
.sp
If defined, only certificates provided by this file will be used:
\fBweb.cacerts\fP and any system/default certificates will not be
used.
.sp
This option has no effect if the per\-host \fBfingerprints\fP option
is set.
.sp
The format of the file is as follows:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
\&... (certificate in base64 PEM encoding) ...
\-\-\-\-\-END CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
\&... (certificate in base64 PEM encoding) ...
\-\-\-\-\-END CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[hostsecurity]
hg.example.com:fingerprints = sha256:c3ab8ff13720e8ad9047dd39466b3c8974e592c2fa383d4a3960714caef0c4f2
hg2.example.com:fingerprints = sha1:914f1aff87249c09b6859b88b1906d30756491ca, sha1:fc:e2:8d:d9:51:cd:cb:c1:4d:18:6b:b7:44:8d:49:72:57:e6:cd:33
hg3.example.com:fingerprints = sha256:9a:b0:dc:e2:75:ad:8a:b7:84:58:e5:1f:07:32:f1:87:e6:bd:24:22:af:b7:ce:8e:9c:b4:10:cf:b9:f4:0e:d2
foo.example.com:verifycertsfile = /etc/ssl/trusted\-ca\-certs.pem
.ft P
.fi
.sp
To change the default minimum protocol version to TLS 1.2 but to allow TLS 1.1
when connecting to \fBhg.example.com\fP:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[hostsecurity]
minimumprotocol = tls1.2
hg.example.com:minimumprotocol = tls1.1
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBhttp_proxy\fP
.sp
Used to access web\-based Mercurial repositories through a HTTP
proxy.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBhost\fP
.sp
Host name and (optional) port of the proxy server, for example
"myproxy:8000".
.TP
.B \fBno\fP
.sp
Optional. Comma\-separated list of host names that should bypass
the proxy.
.TP
.B \fBpasswd\fP
.sp
Optional. Password to authenticate with at the proxy server.
.TP
.B \fBuser\fP
.sp
Optional. User name to authenticate with at the proxy server.
.TP
.B \fBalways\fP
.sp
Optional. Always use the proxy, even for localhost and any entries
in \fBhttp_proxy.no\fP. (default: False)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBhttp\fP
.sp
Used to configure access to Mercurial repositories via HTTP.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBtimeout\fP
.sp
If set, blocking operations will timeout after that many seconds.
(default: None)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBmerge\fP
.sp
This section specifies behavior during merges and updates.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBcheckignored\fP
.sp
Controls behavior when an ignored file on disk has the same name as a tracked
file in the changeset being merged or updated to, and has different
contents. Options are \fBabort\fP, \fBwarn\fP and \fBignore\fP. With \fBabort\fP,
abort on such files. With \fBwarn\fP, warn on such files and back them up as
\fB.orig\fP. With \fBignore\fP, don\(aqt print a warning and back them up as
\fB.orig\fP. (default: \fBabort\fP)
.TP
.B \fBcheckunknown\fP
.sp
Controls behavior when an unknown file that isn\(aqt ignored has the same name
as a tracked file in the changeset being merged or updated to, and has
different contents. Similar to \fBmerge.checkignored\fP, except for files that
are not ignored. (default: \fBabort\fP)
.TP
.B \fBon\-failure\fP
.sp
When set to \fBcontinue\fP (the default), the merge process attempts to
merge all unresolved files using the merge chosen tool, regardless of
whether previous file merge attempts during the process succeeded or not.
Setting this to \fBprompt\fP will prompt after any merge failure continue
or halt the merge process. Setting this to \fBhalt\fP will automatically
halt the merge process on any merge tool failure. The merge process
can be restarted by using the \fBresolve\fP command. When a merge is
halted, the repository is left in a normal \fBunresolved\fP merge state.
(default: \fBcontinue\fP)
.TP
.B \fBstrict\-capability\-check\fP
.sp
Whether capabilities of internal merge tools are checked strictly
or not, while examining rules to decide merge tool to be used.
(default: False)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBmerge\-patterns\fP
.sp
This section specifies merge tools to associate with particular file
patterns. Tools matched here will take precedence over the default
merge tool. Patterns are globs by default, rooted at the repository
root.
.sp
Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[merge\-patterns]
**.c = kdiff3
**.jpg = myimgmerge
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBmerge\-tools\fP
.sp
This section configures external merge tools to use for file\-level
merges. This section has likely been preconfigured at install time.
Use \%\fBhg config merge\-tools\fP\: to check the existing configuration.
Also see \%\fBhg help merge\-tools\fP\: for more details.
.sp
Example \fB~/.hgrc\fP:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[merge\-tools]
# Override stock tool location
kdiff3.executable = ~/bin/kdiff3
# Specify command line
kdiff3.args = $base $local $other \-o $output
# Give higher priority
kdiff3.priority = 1

# Changing the priority of preconfigured tool
meld.priority = 0

# Disable a preconfigured tool
vimdiff.disabled = yes

# Define new tool
myHtmlTool.args = \-m $local $other $base $output
myHtmlTool.regkey = Software\eFooSoftware\eHtmlMerge
myHtmlTool.priority = 1
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Supported arguments:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBpriority\fP
.sp
The priority in which to evaluate this tool.
(default: 0)
.TP
.B \fBexecutable\fP
.sp
Either just the name of the executable or its pathname.
.sp
On Windows, the path can use environment variables with ${ProgramFiles}
syntax.
.sp
(default: the tool name)
.TP
.B \fBargs\fP
.sp
The arguments to pass to the tool executable. You can refer to the
files being merged as well as the output file through these
variables: \fB$base\fP, \fB$local\fP, \fB$other\fP, \fB$output\fP.
.sp
The meaning of \fB$local\fP and \fB$other\fP can vary depending on which action is
being performed. During an update or merge, \fB$local\fP represents the original
state of the file, while \fB$other\fP represents the commit you are updating to or
the commit you are merging with. During a rebase, \fB$local\fP represents the
destination of the rebase, and \fB$other\fP represents the commit being rebased.
.sp
Some operations define custom labels to assist with identifying the revisions,
accessible via \fB$labellocal\fP, \fB$labelother\fP, and \fB$labelbase\fP. If custom
labels are not available, these will be \fBlocal\fP, \fBother\fP, and \fBbase\fP,
respectively.
(default: \fB$local $base $other\fP)
.TP
.B \fBpremerge\fP
.sp
Attempt to run internal non\-interactive 3\-way merge tool before
launching external tool.  Options are \fBtrue\fP, \fBfalse\fP, \fBkeep\fP,
\fBkeep\-merge3\fP, or \fBkeep\-mergediff\fP (experimental). The \fBkeep\fP option
will leave markers in the file if the premerge fails. The \fBkeep\-merge3\fP
will do the same but include information about the base of the merge in the
marker (see internal :merge3 in \%\fBhg help merge\-tools\fP\:). The
\fBkeep\-mergediff\fP option is similar but uses a different marker style
(see internal :merge3 in \%\fBhg help merge\-tools\fP\:). (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBbinary\fP
.sp
This tool can merge binary files. (default: False, unless tool
was selected by file pattern match)
.TP
.B \fBsymlink\fP
.sp
This tool can merge symlinks. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBcheck\fP
.sp
A list of merge success\-checking options:
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBchanged\fP
.sp
Ask whether merge was successful when the merged file shows no changes.
.TP
.B \fBconflicts\fP
.sp
Check whether there are conflicts even though the tool reported success.
.TP
.B \fBprompt\fP
.sp
Always prompt for merge success, regardless of success reported by tool.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBfixeol\fP
.sp
Attempt to fix up EOL changes caused by the merge tool.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBgui\fP
.sp
This tool requires a graphical interface to run. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBmergemarkers\fP
.sp
Controls whether the labels passed via \fB$labellocal\fP, \fB$labelother\fP, and
\fB$labelbase\fP are \fBdetailed\fP (respecting \fBmergemarkertemplate\fP) or
\fBbasic\fP. If \fBpremerge\fP is \fBkeep\fP or \fBkeep\-merge3\fP, the conflict
markers generated during premerge will be \fBdetailed\fP if either this option or
the corresponding option in the \fB[ui]\fP section is \fBdetailed\fP.
(default: \fBbasic\fP)
.TP
.B \fBmergemarkertemplate\fP
.sp
This setting can be used to override \fBmergemarker\fP from the
\fB[command\-templates]\fP section on a per\-tool basis; this applies to the
\fB$label\fP\-prefixed variables and to the conflict markers that are generated
if \fBpremerge\fP is \fBkeep\(ga or \(ga\(gakeep\-merge3\fP. See the corresponding variable
in \fB[ui]\fP for more information.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBregkey\fP
.sp
Windows registry key which describes install location of this
tool. Mercurial will search for this key first under
\fBHKEY_CURRENT_USER\fP and then under \fBHKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\fP.
(default: None)
.TP
.B \fBregkeyalt\fP
.sp
An alternate Windows registry key to try if the first key is not
found.  The alternate key uses the same \fBregname\fP and \fBregappend\fP
semantics of the primary key.  The most common use for this key
is to search for 32bit applications on 64bit operating systems.
(default: None)
.TP
.B \fBregname\fP
.sp
Name of value to read from specified registry key.
(default: the unnamed (default) value)
.TP
.B \fBregappend\fP
.sp
String to append to the value read from the registry, typically
the executable name of the tool.
(default: None)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBpager\fP
.sp
Setting used to control when to paginate and with what external tool. See
\%\fBhg help pager\fP\: for details.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBpager\fP
.sp
Define the external tool used as pager.
.sp
If no pager is set, Mercurial uses the environment variable $PAGER.
If neither pager.pager, nor $PAGER is set, a default pager will be
used, typically \fIless\fP on Unix and \fImore\fP on Windows. Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[pager]
pager = less \-FRX
.ft P
.fi
.TP
.B \fBignore\fP
.sp
List of commands to disable the pager for. Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[pager]
ignore = version, help, update
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBpatch\fP
.sp
Settings used when applying patches, for instance through the \(aqimport\(aq
command or with Mercurial Queues extension.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBeol\fP
.sp
When set to \(aqstrict\(aq patch content and patched files end of lines
are preserved. When set to \fBlf\fP or \fBcrlf\fP, both files end of
lines are ignored when patching and the result line endings are
normalized to either LF (Unix) or CRLF (Windows). When set to
\fBauto\fP, end of lines are again ignored while patching but line
endings in patched files are normalized to their original setting
on a per\-file basis. If target file does not exist or has no end
of line, patch line endings are preserved.
(default: strict)
.TP
.B \fBfuzz\fP
.sp
The number of lines of \(aqfuzz\(aq to allow when applying patches. This
controls how much context the patcher is allowed to ignore when
trying to apply a patch.
(default: 2)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBpaths\fP
.sp
Assigns symbolic names and behavior to repositories.
.sp
Options are symbolic names defining the URL or directory that is the
location of the repository. Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[paths]
my_server = https://example.com/my_repo
local_path = /home/me/repo
.ft P
.fi
.sp
These symbolic names can be used from the command line. To pull
from \fBmy_server\fP: \%\fBhg pull my_server\fP\:. To push to \fBlocal_path\fP:
\%\fBhg push local_path\fP\:. You can check \%\fBhg help urls\fP\: for details about
valid URLs.
.sp
Options containing colons (\fB:\fP) denote sub\-options that can influence
behavior for that specific path. Example:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[paths]
my_server = https://example.com/my_path
my_server:pushurl = ssh://example.com/my_path
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Paths using the \fIpath://otherpath\fP scheme will inherit the sub\-options value from
the path they point to.
.sp
The following sub\-options can be defined:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBmulti\-urls\fP
.sp
A boolean option. When enabled the value of the \fI[paths]\fP entry will be
parsed as a list and the alias will resolve to multiple destination. If some
of the list entry use the \fIpath://\fP syntax, the suboption will be inherited
individually.
.TP
.B \fBpushurl\fP
.sp
The URL to use for push operations. If not defined, the location
defined by the path\(aqs main entry is used.
.TP
.B \fBpushrev\fP
.sp
A revset defining which revisions to push by default.
.sp
When \%\fBhg push\fP\: is executed without a \fB\-r\fP argument, the revset
defined by this sub\-option is evaluated to determine what to push.
.sp
For example, a value of \fB.\fP will push the working directory\(aqs
revision by default.
.sp
Revsets specifying bookmarks will not result in the bookmark being
pushed.
.TP
.B \fBbookmarks.mode\fP
.sp
How bookmark will be dealt during the exchange. It support the following value
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBdefault\fP: the default behavior, local and remote bookmarks are "merged"
on push/pull.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBmirror\fP: when pulling, replace local bookmarks by remote bookmarks. This
is useful to replicate a repository, or as an optimization.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBignore\fP: ignore bookmarks during exchange.
(This currently only affect pulling)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBpulled\-delta\-reuse\-policy\fP
Control the policy regarding deltas sent by the remote during pulls.
.sp
This is an advanced option that non\-admin users should not need to understand
or set. This option can be used to speed up pulls from trusted central
servers, or to fix\-up deltas from older servers.
.sp
It supports the following values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBdefault\fP: use the policy defined by
\fIstorage.revlog.reuse\-external\-delta\-parent\fP,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBno\-reuse\fP: start a new optimal delta search for each new revision we add
to the repository. The deltas from the server will be reused when the base
it applies to is tested (this can be frequent if that base is the one and
unique parent of that revision). This can significantly slowdown pulls but
will result in an optimized storage space if the remote peer is sending poor
quality deltas.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBtry\-base\fP: try to reuse the deltas from the remote peer as long as they
create a valid delta\-chain in the local repository. This speeds up the
unbundling process, but can result in sub\-optimal storage space if the
remote peer is sending poor quality deltas.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBforced\fP: the deltas from the peer will be reused in all cases, even if
the resulting delta\-chain is "invalid". This setting will ensure the bundle
is applied at minimal CPU cost, but it can result in longer delta chains
being created on the client, making revisions potentially slower to access
in the future. If you think you need this option, you should make sure you
are also talking to the Mercurial developer community to get confirmation.
.UNINDENT
.sp
See \fIhg help config.storage.revlog.reuse\-external\-delta\-parent\fP for a similar
global option. That option defines the behavior of \fIdefault\fP.
.sp
The following special named paths exist:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBdefault\fP
.sp
The URL or directory to use when no source or remote is specified.
.sp
\%\fBhg clone\fP\: will automatically define this path to the location the
repository was cloned from.
.TP
.B \fBdefault\-push\fP
.sp
(deprecated) The URL or directory for the default \%\fBhg push\fP\: location.
\fBdefault:pushurl\fP should be used instead.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBphases\fP
.sp
Specifies default handling of phases. See \%\fBhg help phases\fP\: for more
information about working with phases.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBpublish\fP
.sp
Controls draft phase behavior when working as a server. When true,
pushed changesets are set to public in both client and server and
pulled or cloned changesets are set to public in the client.
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBnew\-commit\fP
.sp
Phase of newly\-created commits.
(default: draft)
.TP
.B \fBchecksubrepos\fP
.sp
Check the phase of the current revision of each subrepository. Allowed
values are "ignore", "follow" and "abort". For settings other than
"ignore", the phase of the current revision of each subrepository is
checked before committing the parent repository. If any of those phases is
greater than the phase of the parent repository (e.g. if a subrepo is in a
"secret" phase while the parent repo is in "draft" phase), the commit is
either aborted (if checksubrepos is set to "abort") or the higher phase is
used for the parent repository commit (if set to "follow").
(default: follow)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBprofiling\fP
.sp
Specifies profiling type, format, and file output. Two profilers are
supported: an instrumenting profiler (named \fBls\fP), and a sampling
profiler (named \fBstat\fP).
.sp
In this section description, \(aqprofiling data\(aq stands for the raw data
collected during profiling, while \(aqprofiling report\(aq stands for a
statistical text report generated from the profiling data.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBenabled\fP
.sp
Enable the profiler.
(default: false)
.sp
This is equivalent to passing \fB\-\-profile\fP on the command line.
.TP
.B \fBtype\fP
.sp
The type of profiler to use.
(default: stat)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBls\fP
.sp
Use Python\(aqs built\-in instrumenting profiler. This profiler
works on all platforms, but each line number it reports is the
first line of a function. This restriction makes it difficult to
identify the expensive parts of a non\-trivial function.
.TP
.B \fBstat\fP
.sp
Use a statistical profiler, statprof. This profiler is most
useful for profiling commands that run for longer than about 0.1
seconds.
.TP
.B \fBpy\-spy\fP
.sp
use the py\-spy profiler. A external py\-spy executable must be available.
(Make sure to check \fIprofiling.output\fP config to write the result.)
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBformat\fP
.sp
Profiling format.  Specific to the \fBls\fP instrumenting profiler.
(default: text)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBtext\fP
.sp
Generate a profiling report. When saving to a file, it should be
noted that only the report is saved, and the profiling data is
not kept.
.TP
.B \fBkcachegrind\fP
.sp
Format profiling data for kcachegrind use: when saving to a
file, the generated file can directly be loaded into
kcachegrind.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBstatformat\fP
.sp
Profiling format for the \fBstat\fP profiler.
(default: hotpath)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBhotpath\fP
.sp
Show a tree\-based display containing the hot path of execution (where
most time was spent).
.TP
.B \fBbymethod\fP
.sp
Show a table of methods ordered by how frequently they are active.
.TP
.B \fBbyline\fP
.sp
Show a table of lines in files ordered by how frequently they are active.
.TP
.B \fBjson\fP
.sp
Render profiling data as JSON.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBfreq\fP
.sp
Sampling frequency.  Specific to the \fBstat\fP sampling profiler.
(default: 1000)
.TP
.B \fBoutput\fP
.sp
File path where profiling data or report should be saved. If the
file exists, it is replaced. (default: None, data is printed on
stderr)
.TP
.B \fBoutput\-dir\fP
.sp
Directory in which profiling data or report should be saved. The data will be written to a file with the naming scheme "hg\-profile\-{TIMESTAMP}\-{PID}.prof". If both \fBoutput\fP and \fBoutput\-dir\fP are specified, \fBoutput\fP will take precedence.
.TP
.B \fBoutput\-dir:create\fP
.sp
Create output\-dir if it doesn\(aqt already exist.
.TP
.B \fBsort\fP
.sp
Sort field.  Specific to the \fBls\fP instrumenting profiler.
One of \fBcallcount\fP, \fBreccallcount\fP, \fBtotaltime\fP and
\fBinlinetime\fP.
(default: inlinetime)
.TP
.B \fBtime\-track\fP
.sp
Control if the stat profiler track \fBcpu\fP or \fBreal\fP time.
(default: \fBcpu\fP on Windows, otherwise \fBreal\fP)
.TP
.B \fBlimit\fP
.sp
Number of lines to show. Specific to the \fBls\fP instrumenting profiler.
(default: 30)
.TP
.B \fBnested\fP
.sp
Show at most this number of lines of drill\-down info after each main entry.
This can help explain the difference between Total and Inline.
Specific to the \fBls\fP instrumenting profiler.
(default: 0)
.TP
.B \fBshowmin\fP
.sp
Minimum fraction of samples an entry must have for it to be displayed.
Can be specified as a float between \fB0.0\fP and \fB1.0\fP or can have a
\fB%\fP afterwards to allow values up to \fB100\fP. e.g. \fB5%\fP.
.sp
Only used by the \fBstat\fP profiler.
.sp
For the \fBhotpath\fP format, default is \fB0.05\fP.
For the \fBchrome\fP format, default is \fB0.005\fP.
.sp
The option is unused on other formats.
.TP
.B \fBshowmax\fP
.sp
Maximum fraction of samples an entry can have before it is ignored in
display. Values format is the same as \fBshowmin\fP.
.sp
Only used by the \fBstat\fP profiler.
.sp
For the \fBchrome\fP format, default is \fB0.999\fP.
.sp
The option is unused on other formats.
.TP
.B \fBshowtime\fP
.sp
Show time taken as absolute durations, in addition to percentages.
Only used by the \fBhotpath\fP format.
(default: true)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBprogress\fP
.sp
Mercurial commands can draw progress bars that are as informative as
possible. Some progress bars only offer indeterminate information, while others
have a definite end point.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBdebug\fP
.sp
Whether to print debug info when updating the progress bar. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBdelay\fP
.sp
Number of seconds (float) before showing the progress bar. (default: 3)
.TP
.B \fBchangedelay\fP
.sp
Minimum delay before showing a new topic. When set to less than 3 * refresh,
that value will be used instead. (default: 1)
.TP
.B \fBestimateinterval\fP
.sp
Maximum sampling interval in seconds for speed and estimated time
calculation. (default: 60)
.TP
.B \fBrefresh\fP
.sp
Time in seconds between refreshes of the progress bar. (default: 0.1)
.TP
.B \fBformat\fP
.sp
Format of the progress bar.
.sp
Valid entries for the format field are \fBtopic\fP, \fBbar\fP, \fBnumber\fP,
\fBunit\fP, \fBestimate\fP, \fBspeed\fP, and \fBitem\fP. \fBitem\fP defaults to the
last 20 characters of the item, but this can be changed by adding either
\fB\-<num>\fP which would take the last num characters, or \fB+<num>\fP for the
first num characters.
.sp
(default: topic bar number estimate)
.TP
.B \fBwidth\fP
.sp
If set, the maximum width of the progress information (that is, min(width,
term width) will be used).
.TP
.B \fBclear\-complete\fP
.sp
Clear the progress bar after it\(aqs done. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBdisable\fP
.sp
If true, don\(aqt show a progress bar.
.TP
.B \fBassume\-tty\fP
.sp
If true, ALWAYS show a progress bar, unless disable is given.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBrebase\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBevolution.allowdivergence\fP
.sp
Default to False, when True allow creating divergence when performing
rebase of obsolete changesets.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBrevsetalias\fP
.sp
Alias definitions for revsets. See \%\fBhg help revsets\fP\: for details.
.SS \fBrewrite\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBbackup\-bundle\fP
.sp
Whether to save stripped changesets to a bundle file. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBupdate\-timestamp\fP
.sp
If true, updates the date and time of the changeset to current. It is only
applicable for \fIhg amend\fP, \fIhg commit \-\-amend\fP and \fIhg uncommit\fP in the
current version.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBempty\-successor\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
Control what happens with empty successors that are the result of rewrite
operations. If set to \fBskip\fP, the successor is not created. If set to
\fBkeep\fP, the empty successor is created and kept.
.sp
Currently, only the rebase and absorb commands consider this configuration.
(EXPERIMENTAL)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBrhg\fP
.sp
The pure Rust fast\-path for Mercurial. See \fIrust/README.md\fP in the Mercurial repository.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBfallback\-executable\fP
.sp
Path to the executable to run in a sub\-process when falling back to
another implementation of Mercurial.
.TP
.B \fBfallback\-immediately\fP
.sp
Fall back to \fBfallback\-executable\fP as soon as possible, regardless of
the \fIrhg.on\-unsupported\fP configuration. Useful for debugging, for example to
bypass \fIrhg\fP if the deault \fIhg\fP points to \fIrhg\fP.
.sp
Note that because this requires loading the configuration, it is possible
that \fIrhg\fP error out before being able to fall back.
.TP
.B \fBignored\-extensions\fP
.sp
Controls which extensions should be ignored by \fIrhg\fP. By default, \fIrhg\fP
triggers the \fIrhg.on\-unsupported\fP behavior any unsupported extensions.
Users can disable that behavior when they know that a given extension
does not need support from \fIrhg\fP.
.sp
Expects a list of extension names, or \fB*\fP to ignore all extensions.
.sp
Note: \fB*:<suboption>\fP is also a valid extension name for this
configuration option.
As of this writing, the only valid "global" suboption is \fBrequired\fP.
.TP
.B \fBon\-unsupported\fP
.sp
Controls the behavior of \fIrhg\fP when detecting unsupported features.
.sp
Possible values are \fIabort\fP (default), \fIabort\-silent\fP and \fIfallback\fP.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBabort\fP
.sp
Print an error message describing what feature is not supported,
and exit with code 252
.TP
.B \fBabort\-silent\fP
.sp
Silently exit with code 252
.TP
.B \fBfallback\fP
.sp
Try running the fallback executable with the same parameters
(and trace the fallback reason, use \fIRUST_LOG=trace\fP to see).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBshare\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBsafe\-mismatch.source\-safe\fP
.sp
Controls what happens when the shared repository does not use the
share\-safe mechanism but its source repository does.
.sp
Possible values are \fIabort\fP (default), \fIallow\fP, \fIupgrade\-abort\fP and
\fIupgrade\-allow\fP.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBabort\fP
.sp
Disallows running any command and aborts
.TP
.B \fBallow\fP
.sp
Respects the feature presence in the share source
.TP
.B \fBupgrade\-abort\fP
.sp
Tries to upgrade the share to use share\-safe; if it fails, aborts
.TP
.B \fBupgrade\-allow\fP
.sp
Tries to upgrade the share; if it fails, continue by
respecting the share source setting
.UNINDENT
.sp
Check \%\fBhg help config.format.use\-share\-safe\fP\: for details about the
share\-safe feature.
.TP
.B \fBsafe\-mismatch.source\-safe:verbose\-upgrade\fP
.sp
Display a message when upgrading, (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBsafe\-mismatch.source\-safe.warn\fP
.sp
Shows a warning on operations if the shared repository does not use
share\-safe, but the source repository does.
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBsafe\-mismatch.source\-not\-safe\fP
.sp
Controls what happens when the shared repository uses the share\-safe
mechanism but its source does not.
.sp
Possible values are \fIabort\fP (default), \fIallow\fP, \fIdowngrade\-abort\fP and
\fIdowngrade\-allow\fP.
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBabort\fP
.sp
Disallows running any command and aborts
.TP
.B \fBallow\fP
.sp
Respects the feature presence in the share source
.TP
.B \fBdowngrade\-abort\fP
.sp
Tries to downgrade the share to not use share\-safe; if it fails, aborts
.TP
.B \fBdowngrade\-allow\fP
.sp
Tries to downgrade the share to not use share\-safe;
if it fails, continue by respecting the shared source setting
.UNINDENT
.sp
Check \%\fBhg help config.format.use\-share\-safe\fP\: for details about the
share\-safe feature.
.TP
.B \fBsafe\-mismatch.source\-not\-safe:verbose\-upgrade\fP
.sp
Display a message when upgrading, (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBsafe\-mismatch.source\-not\-safe.warn\fP
.sp
Shows a warning on operations if the shared repository uses share\-safe,
but the source repository does not.
(default: True)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBstorage\fP
.sp
Control the strategy Mercurial uses internally to store history. Options in this
category impact performance and repository size.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBall\-slow\-path\fP
.sp
Control the behavior of Mercurial when lacking performant code
.sp
Some format features might only have a fast implementation when a specific
flavor is installed (e.g. when the Rust extensions are available). A slow pure
Python implementation might be available to help with converting the repository
to another format or to perform a few accesses in an automation context.
Using such accesses might result in Mercurial becoming multiple time slower
than usual.
.sp
Acceptable values:
.sp
\fBallow\fP: Silently use the slower implementation to access the repository.
\fBwarn\fP: Warn, but use the slower implementation to access the repository.
\fBabort\fP: Prevent access to such repositories. (This is the default)
.sp
See the individual "storage.*.slow\-path" configs for details. These options can
override the default.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.dirstate\-v2.slow\-path\fP
.sp
Control the behavior of Mercurial when using a repository with the "dirstate\-v2"
feature with an installation of Mercurial without a fast implementation for
the feature:
.sp
\fBallow\fP: Silently use the slower implementation to access the repository.
\fBwarn\fP: Warn, but use the slower implementation to access the repository.
\fBdefault\fP: use value from the \fIstorage.all\-slow\-path\fP value.
\fBabort\fP: Prevent access to such repositories. (This is the default)
.sp
For details on the "dirstate\-v2" feature, see:
\%\fBhg help config.format.use\-dirstate\-v2\fP\:.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.exchange\-compressed\-delta\fP
.sp
Controls whether the repository might emit or request
compressed deltas in bundles.
.sp
Exchanging deltas in their compressed form results in larger bundles and higher
bandwidth usage, but reduces CPU usage on both ends and helps the main server
to use agressive compression levels to share that benefit with ordinary clients
pulling from them.
.sp
Even when activated, the exchange of compressed deltas will only be used if the
exchange uses a changegroup format that supports it (04 and above) and the
receiving end supports it.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.issue6528.fix\-incoming\fP
.sp
Version 5.8 of Mercurial had a bug leading to altering the parent of file
revision with copy information (or any other metadata) on exchange. This
leads to the copy metadata to be overlooked by various internal logic. The
issue was fixed in Mercurial 5.8.1.
(See \%https://bz.mercurial\-scm.org/show_bug.cgi?id=6528\: for details)
.sp
As a result Mercurial is now checking and fixing incoming file revisions to
make sure there parents are in the right order. This behavior can be
disabled by setting this option to \fIno\fP. This apply to revisions added
through push, pull, clone and unbundle.
.sp
To fix affected revisions that already exist within the repository, one can
use \%\fBhg debug\-repair\-issue\-6528\fP\:.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.delta\-parent\-search.candidate\-group\-chunk\-size\fP
.sp
Tune the number of delta bases the storage will consider in the
same "round" of search. In some very rare cases, using a smaller value
might result in faster processing at the possible expense of storage
space, while using larger values might result in slower processing at the
possible benefit of storage space. A value of "0" means no limitation.
.sp
default: no limitation
.sp
This is unlikely that you\(aqll have to tune this configuration. If you think
you do, consider talking with the mercurial developer community about your
repositories.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.mmap.index\fP
.sp
Whether to use the Operating System "memory mapping" feature (when
possible) to access the revlog index. This improves performance
and reduces memory pressure.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBrevlog.mmap.index:size\-threshold\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
The size of index above which to use the "memory mapping" feature.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.optimize\-delta\-parent\-choice\fP
.sp
When storing a merge revision, both parents will be equally considered as
a possible delta base. This results in better delta selection and improved
revlog compression. This option is enabled by default.
.sp
Turning this option off can result in large increase of repository size for
repository with many merges.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.persistent\-nodemap.mmap\fP
.sp
Whether to use the Operating System "memory mapping" feature (when
possible) to access the persistent nodemap data. This improve performance
and reduce memory pressure.
.sp
Default to True.
.sp
For details on the "persistent\-nodemap" feature, see:
\%\fBhg help config.format.use\-persistent\-nodemap\fP\:.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.persistent\-nodemap.slow\-path\fP
.sp
Control the behavior of Mercurial when using a repository with a "persistent"
nodemap with an installation of Mercurial without a fast implementation for
the feature:
.sp
\fBallow\fP: Silently use the slower implementation to access the repository.
\fBwarn\fP: Warn, but use the slower implementation to access the repository.
\fBdefault\fP: use the value from the \fIstorage.all\-slow\-path\fP value.
\fBabort\fP: Prevent access to such repositories. (This is the default)
.sp
For details on the "persistent\-nodemap" feature, see:
\%\fBhg help config.format.use\-persistent\-nodemap\fP\:.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.reuse\-external\-delta\-parent\fP
.sp
Control the order in which delta parents are considered when adding new
revisions from an external source.
(typically: apply bundle from \fIhg pull\fP or \fIhg push\fP).
.sp
New revisions are usually provided as a delta against other revisions. By
default, Mercurial will try to reuse this delta first, therefore using the
same "delta parent" as the source. Directly using delta\(aqs from the source
reduces CPU usage and usually speeds up operation. However, in some case,
the source might have sub\-optimal delta bases and forcing their reevaluation
is useful. For example, pushes from an old client could have sub\-optimal
delta\(aqs parent that the server want to optimize. (lack of general delta, bad
parents, choice, lack of sparse\-revlog, etc).
.sp
This option is enabled by default. Turning it off will ensure bad delta
parent choices from older client do not propagate to this repository, at
the cost of a small increase in CPU consumption.
.sp
Note: this option only control the order in which delta parents are
considered.  Even when disabled, the existing delta from the source will be
reused if the same delta parent is selected.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.reuse\-external\-delta\fP
.sp
Control the reuse of delta from external source.
(typically: apply bundle from \fIhg pull\fP or \fIhg push\fP).
.sp
New revisions are usually provided as a delta against another revision. By
default, Mercurial will not recompute the same delta again, trusting
externally provided deltas. There have been rare cases of small adjustment
to the diffing algorithm in the past. So in some rare case, recomputing
delta provided by ancient clients can provides better results. Disabling
this option means going through a full delta recomputation for all incoming
revisions. It means a large increase in CPU usage and will slow operations
down.
.sp
This option is enabled by default. When disabled, it also disables the
related \fBstorage.revlog.reuse\-external\-delta\-parent\fP option.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.reuse\-external\-delta\-compression\fP
.sp
When true, reuse the compression of incoming deltas
when receiving a compressed delta if its compression type is compatible
with the one used by the revlog.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.zlib.level\fP
.sp
Zlib compression level used when storing data into the repository. Accepted
Value range from 1 (lowest compression) to 9 (highest compression). Zlib
default value is 6.
.TP
.B \fBrevlog.zstd.level\fP
.sp
zstd compression level used when storing data into the repository. Accepted
Value range from 1 (lowest compression) to 22 (highest compression).
(default 3)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBserver\fP
.sp
Controls generic server settings.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBbookmarks\-pushkey\-compat\fP
.sp
Trigger pushkey hook when being pushed bookmark updates. This config exist
for compatibility purpose (default to True)
.sp
If you use \fBpushkey\fP and \fBpre\-pushkey\fP hooks to control bookmark
movement we recommend you migrate them to \fBtxnclose\-bookmark\fP and
\fBpretxnclose\-bookmark\fP.
.TP
.B \fBcompressionengines\fP
.sp
List of compression engines and their relative priority to advertise
to clients.
.sp
The order of compression engines determines their priority, the first
having the highest priority. If a compression engine is not listed
here, it won\(aqt be advertised to clients.
.sp
If not set (the default), built\-in defaults are used. Run
\%\fBhg debuginstall\fP\: to list available compression engines and their
default wire protocol priority.
.sp
Older Mercurial clients only support zlib compression and this setting
has no effect for legacy clients.
.TP
.B \fBuncompressed\fP
.sp
Whether to allow clients to clone a repository using the
uncompressed streaming protocol. This transfers about 40% more
data than a regular clone, but uses less memory and CPU on both
server and client. Over a LAN (100 Mbps or better) or a very fast
WAN, an uncompressed streaming clone is a lot faster (~10x) than a
regular clone. Over most WAN connections (anything slower than
about 6 Mbps), uncompressed streaming is slower, because of the
extra data transfer overhead. This mode will also temporarily hold
the write lock while determining what data to transfer.
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBuncompressedallowsecret\fP
.sp
Whether to allow stream clones when the repository contains secret
changesets. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBpreferuncompressed\fP
.sp
When set, clients will try to use the uncompressed streaming
protocol. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBdisablefullbundle\fP
.sp
When set, servers will refuse attempts to do pull\-based clones.
If this option is set, \fBpreferuncompressed\fP and/or clone bundles
are highly recommended. Partial clones will still be allowed.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBstreamunbundle\fP
.sp
When set, servers will apply data sent from the client directly,
otherwise it will be written to a temporary file first. This option
effectively prevents concurrent pushes.
.TP
.B \fBpullbundle\fP
.sp
When set, the server will check pullbundles.manifest for bundles
covering the requested heads and common nodes. The first matching
entry will be streamed to the client.
.sp
For HTTP transport, the stream will still use zlib compression
for older clients.
.TP
.B \fBconcurrent\-push\-mode\fP
.sp
Level of allowed race condition between two pushing clients.
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\(aqstrict\(aq: push is abort if another client touched the repository
while the push was preparing.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\(aqcheck\-related\(aq: push is only aborted if it affects head that got also
affected while the push was preparing. (default since 5.4)
.UNINDENT
.sp
\(aqcheck\-related\(aq only takes effect for compatible clients (version
4.3 and later). Older clients will use \(aqstrict\(aq.
.TP
.B \fBvalidate\fP
.sp
Whether to validate the completeness of pushed changesets by
checking that all new file revisions specified in manifests are
present. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBmaxhttpheaderlen\fP
.sp
Instruct HTTP clients not to send request headers longer than this
many bytes. (default: 1024)
.TP
.B \fBbundle1\fP
.sp
Whether to allow clients to push and pull using the legacy bundle1
exchange format. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBbundle1gd\fP
.sp
Like \fBbundle1\fP but only used if the repository is using the
\fIgeneraldelta\fP storage format. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBbundle1.push\fP
.sp
Whether to allow clients to push using the legacy bundle1 exchange
format. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBbundle1gd.push\fP
.sp
Like \fBbundle1.push\fP but only used if the repository is using the
\fIgeneraldelta\fP storage format. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBbundle1.pull\fP
.sp
Whether to allow clients to pull using the legacy bundle1 exchange
format. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBbundle1gd.pull\fP
.sp
Like \fBbundle1.pull\fP but only used if the repository is using the
\fIgeneraldelta\fP storage format. (default: True)
.sp
Large repositories using the \fIgeneraldelta\fP storage format should
consider setting this option because converting \fIgeneraldelta\fP
repositories to the exchange format required by the bundle1 data
format can consume a lot of CPU.
.TP
.B \fBbundle2.stream\fP
.sp
Whether to allow clients to pull using the bundle2 streaming protocol.
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBzliblevel\fP
.sp
Integer between \fB\-1\fP and \fB9\fP that controls the zlib compression level
for wire protocol commands that send zlib compressed output (notably the
commands that send repository history data).
.sp
The default (\fB\-1\fP) uses the default zlib compression level, which is
likely equivalent to \fB6\fP. \fB0\fP means no compression. \fB9\fP means
maximum compression.
.sp
Setting this option allows server operators to make trade\-offs between
bandwidth and CPU used. Lowering the compression lowers CPU utilization
but sends more bytes to clients.
.sp
This option only impacts the HTTP server.
.TP
.B \fBzstdlevel\fP
.sp
Integer between \fB1\fP and \fB22\fP that controls the zstd compression level
for wire protocol commands. \fB1\fP is the minimal amount of compression and
\fB22\fP is the highest amount of compression.
.sp
The default (\fB3\fP) should be significantly faster than zlib while likely
delivering better compression ratios.
.sp
This option only impacts the HTTP server.
.sp
See also \fBserver.zliblevel\fP.
.TP
.B \fBview\fP
.sp
Repository filter used when exchanging revisions with the peer.
.sp
The default view (\fBserved\fP) excludes secret and hidden changesets.
Another useful value is \fBimmutable\fP (no draft, secret or hidden
changesets). (EXPERIMENTAL)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBsmtp\fP
.sp
Configuration for extensions that need to send email messages.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBhost\fP
.sp
Host name of mail server, e.g. "mail.example.com".
.TP
.B \fBport\fP
.sp
Optional. Port to connect to on mail server. (default: 465 if
\fBtls\fP is smtps; 25 otherwise)
.TP
.B \fBtls\fP
.sp
Optional. Method to enable TLS when connecting to mail server: starttls,
smtps or none. (default: none)
.TP
.B \fBusername\fP
.sp
Optional. User name for authenticating with the SMTP server.
(default: None)
.TP
.B \fBpassword\fP
.sp
Optional. Password for authenticating with the SMTP server. If not
specified, interactive sessions will prompt the user for a
password; non\-interactive sessions will fail. (default: None)
.TP
.B \fBlocal_hostname\fP
.sp
Optional. The hostname that the sender can use to identify
itself to the MTA.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBsubpaths\fP
.sp
Subrepository source URLs can go stale if a remote server changes name
or becomes temporarily unavailable. This section lets you define
rewrite rules of the form:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
<pattern> = <replacement>
.ft P
.fi
.sp
where \fBpattern\fP is a regular expression matching a subrepository
source URL and \fBreplacement\fP is the replacement string used to
rewrite it. Groups can be matched in \fBpattern\fP and referenced in
\fBreplacements\fP. For instance:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
http://server/(.*)\-hg/ = http://hg.server/\e1/
.ft P
.fi
.sp
rewrites \fBhttp://server/foo\-hg/\fP into \fBhttp://hg.server/foo/\fP.
.sp
Relative subrepository paths are first made absolute, and the
rewrite rules are then applied on the full (absolute) path. If \fBpattern\fP
doesn\(aqt match the full path, an attempt is made to apply it on the
relative path alone. The rules are applied in definition order.
.SS \fBsubrepos\fP
.sp
This section contains options that control the behavior of the
subrepositories feature. See also \%\fBhg help subrepos\fP\:.
.sp
Security note: auditing in Mercurial is known to be insufficient to
prevent clone\-time code execution with carefully constructed Git
subrepos. It is unknown if a similar detect is present in Subversion
subrepos. Both Git and Subversion subrepos are disabled by default
out of security concerns. These subrepo types can be enabled using
the respective options below.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBallowed\fP
.sp
Whether subrepositories are allowed in the working directory.
.sp
When false, commands involving subrepositories (like \%\fBhg update\fP\:)
will fail for all subrepository types.
(default: true)
.TP
.B \fBhg:allowed\fP
.sp
Whether Mercurial subrepositories are allowed in the working
directory. This option only has an effect if \fBsubrepos.allowed\fP
is true.
(default: true)
.TP
.B \fBgit:allowed\fP
.sp
Whether Git subrepositories are allowed in the working directory.
This option only has an effect if \fBsubrepos.allowed\fP is true.
.sp
See the security note above before enabling Git subrepos.
(default: false)
.TP
.B \fBsvn:allowed\fP
.sp
Whether Subversion subrepositories are allowed in the working
directory. This option only has an effect if \fBsubrepos.allowed\fP
is true.
.sp
See the security note above before enabling Subversion subrepos.
(default: false)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBtemplatealias\fP
.sp
Alias definitions for templates. See \%\fBhg help templates\fP\: for details.
.SS \fBtemplates\fP
.sp
Use the \fB[templates]\fP section to define template strings.
See \%\fBhg help templates\fP\: for details.
.SS \fBtrusted\fP
.sp
Mercurial will not use the settings in the
\fB.hg/hgrc\fP file from a repository if it doesn\(aqt belong to a trusted
user or to a trusted group, as various hgrc features allow arbitrary
commands to be run. This issue is often encountered when configuring
hooks or extensions for shared repositories or servers. However,
the web interface will use some safe settings from the \fB[web]\fP
section.
.sp
This section specifies what users and groups are trusted. The
current user is always trusted. To trust everybody, list a user or a
group with name \fB*\fP. These settings must be placed in an
\fIalready\-trusted file\fP to take effect, such as \fB$HOME/.hgrc\fP of the
user or service running Mercurial.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBusers\fP
.sp
Comma\-separated list of trusted users.
.TP
.B \fBgroups\fP
.sp
Comma\-separated list of trusted groups.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBui\fP
.sp
User interface controls.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBarchivemeta\fP
.sp
Whether to include the .hg_archival.txt file containing meta data
(hashes for the repository base and for tip) in archives created
by the \%\fBhg archive\fP\: command or downloaded via hgweb.
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBaskusername\fP
.sp
Whether to prompt for a username when committing. If True, and
neither \fB$HGUSER\fP nor \fB$EMAIL\fP has been specified, then the user will
be prompted to enter a username. If no username is entered, the
default \fBUSER@HOST\fP is used instead.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBclonebundles\fP
.sp
Whether the "clone bundles" feature is enabled.
.sp
When enabled, \%\fBhg clone\fP\: may download and apply a server\-advertised
bundle file from a URL instead of using the normal exchange mechanism.
.sp
This can likely result in faster and more reliable clones.
.sp
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBclonebundlefallback\fP
.sp
Whether failure to apply an advertised "clone bundle" from a server
should result in fallback to a regular clone.
.sp
This is disabled by default because servers advertising "clone
bundles" often do so to reduce server load. If advertised bundles
start mass failing and clients automatically fall back to a regular
clone, this would add significant and unexpected load to the server
since the server is expecting clone operations to be offloaded to
pre\-generated bundles. Failing fast (the default behavior) ensures
clients don\(aqt overwhelm the server when "clone bundle" application
fails.
.sp
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBclonebundleprefers\fP
.sp
Defines preferences for which "clone bundles" to use.
.sp
Servers advertising "clone bundles" may advertise multiple available
bundles. Each bundle may have different attributes, such as the bundle
type and compression format. This option is used to prefer a particular
bundle over another.
.sp
The following keys are defined by Mercurial:
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B BUNDLESPEC
.
A bundle type specifier. These are strings passed to \%\fBhg bundle \-t\fP\:.
e.g. \fBgzip\-v2\fP or \fBbzip2\-v1\fP.
.TP
.B COMPRESSION
.
The compression format of the bundle. e.g. \fBgzip\fP and \fBbzip2\fP.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Server operators may define custom keys.
.sp
Example values: \fBCOMPRESSION=bzip2\fP,
\fBBUNDLESPEC=gzip\-v2, COMPRESSION=gzip\fP.
.sp
By default, the first bundle advertised by the server is used.
.TP
.B \fBcolor\fP
.sp
When to colorize output. Possible value are Boolean ("yes" or "no"), or
"debug", or "always". (default: "yes"). "yes" will use color whenever it
seems possible. See \%\fBhg help color\fP\: for details.
.TP
.B \fBcommitsubrepos\fP
.sp
Whether to commit modified subrepositories when committing the
parent repository. If False and one subrepository has uncommitted
changes, abort the commit.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBdebug\fP
.sp
Print debugging information. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBeditor\fP
.sp
The editor to use during a commit. (default: \fB$EDITOR\fP or \fBvi\fP)
.TP
.B \fBfallbackencoding\fP
.sp
Encoding to try if it\(aqs not possible to decode the changelog using
UTF\-8. (default: ISO\-8859\-1)
.TP
.B \fBgraphnodetemplate\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Use \fBcommand\-templates.graphnode\fP instead.
.TP
.B \fBignore\fP
.sp
A file to read per\-user ignore patterns from. This file should be
in the same format as a repository\-wide .hgignore file. Filenames
are relative to the repository root. This option supports hook syntax,
so if you want to specify multiple ignore files, you can do so by
setting something like \fBignore.other = ~/.hgignore2\fP. For details
of the ignore file format, see the \fBhgignore(5)\fP man page.
.TP
.B \fBinteractive\fP
.sp
Allow to prompt the user. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBinterface\fP
.sp
Select the default interface for interactive features (default: text).
Possible values are \(aqtext\(aq and \(aqcurses\(aq.
.TP
.B \fBinterface.chunkselector\fP
.sp
Select the interface for change recording (e.g. \%\fBhg commit \-i\fP\:).
Possible values are \(aqtext\(aq and \(aqcurses\(aq.
This config overrides the interface specified by ui.interface.
.TP
.B \fBlarge\-file\-limit\fP
.sp
Largest file size that gives no memory use warning.
Possible values are integers or 0 to disable the check.
Value is expressed in bytes by default, one can use standard units for
convenience (e.g. 10MB, 0.1GB, etc) (default: 10MB)
.TP
.B \fBlogtemplate\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Use \fBcommand\-templates.log\fP instead.
.TP
.B \fBmerge\fP
.sp
The conflict resolution program to use during a manual merge.
For more information on merge tools see \%\fBhg help merge\-tools\fP\:.
For configuring merge tools see the \fB[merge\-tools]\fP section.
.TP
.B \fBmergemarkers\fP
.sp
Sets the merge conflict marker label styling. The \fBdetailed\fP style
uses the \fBcommand\-templates.mergemarker\fP setting to style the labels.
The \fBbasic\fP style just uses \(aqlocal\(aq and \(aqother\(aq as the marker label.
One of \fBbasic\fP or \fBdetailed\fP.
(default: \fBbasic\fP)
.TP
.B \fBmergemarkertemplate\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Use \fBcommand\-templates.mergemarker\fP instead.
.TP
.B \fBmessage\-output\fP
.sp
Where to write status and error messages. (default: \fBstdio\fP)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBchannel\fP
.sp
Use separate channel for structured output. (Command\-server only)
.TP
.B \fBstderr\fP
.sp
Everything to stderr.
.TP
.B \fBstdio\fP
.sp
Status to stdout, and error to stderr.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBorigbackuppath\fP
.sp
The path to a directory used to store generated .orig files. If the path is
not a directory, one will be created.  If set, files stored in this
directory have the same name as the original file and do not have a .orig
suffix.
.TP
.B \fBpaginate\fP
.sp
Control the pagination of command output (default: True). See \%\fBhg help pager\fP\:
for details.
.TP
.B \fBpatch\fP
.sp
An optional external tool that \fBhg import\fP and some extensions
will use for applying patches. By default Mercurial uses an
internal patch utility. The external tool must work as the common
Unix \fBpatch\fP program. In particular, it must accept a \fB\-p\fP
argument to strip patch headers, a \fB\-d\fP argument to specify the
current directory, a file name to patch, and a patch file to take
from stdin.
.sp
It is possible to specify a patch tool together with extra
arguments. For example, setting this option to \fBpatch \-\-merge\fP
will use the \fBpatch\fP program with its 2\-way merge option.
.TP
.B \fBportablefilenames\fP
.sp
Check for portable filenames. Can be \fBwarn\fP, \fBignore\fP or \fBabort\fP.
(default: \fBwarn\fP)
.INDENT 7.0
.TP
.B \fBwarn\fP
.sp
Print a warning message on POSIX platforms, if a file with a non\-portable
filename is added (e.g. a file with a name that can\(aqt be created on
Windows because it contains reserved parts like \fBAUX\fP, reserved
characters like \fB:\fP, or would cause a case collision with an existing
file).
.TP
.B \fBignore\fP
.sp
Don\(aqt print a warning.
.TP
.B \fBabort\fP
.sp
The command is aborted.
.TP
.B \fBtrue\fP
.sp
Alias for \fBwarn\fP.
.TP
.B \fBfalse\fP
.sp
Alias for \fBignore\fP.
.UNINDENT
.sp
On Windows, this configuration option is ignored and the command aborted.
.TP
.B \fBpre\-merge\-tool\-output\-template\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Use \fBcommand\-template.pre\-merge\-tool\-output\fP instead.
.TP
.B \fBquiet\fP
.sp
Reduce the amount of output printed.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBrelative\-paths\fP
.sp
Prefer relative paths in the UI.
.TP
.B \fBremotecmd\fP
.sp
Remote command to use for clone/push/pull operations.
(default: \fBhg\fP)
.TP
.B \fBreport_untrusted\fP
.sp
Warn if a \fB.hg/hgrc\fP file is ignored due to not being owned by a
trusted user or group.
(default: True)
.TP
.B \fBslash\fP
.sp
(Deprecated. Use \fBslashpath\fP template filter instead.)
.sp
Display paths using a slash (\fB/\fP) as the path separator. This
only makes a difference on systems where the default path
separator is not the slash character (e.g. Windows uses the
backslash character (\fB\e\fP)).
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBstatuscopies\fP
.sp
Display copies in the status command.
.TP
.B \fBssh\fP
.sp
Command to use for SSH connections. (default: \fBssh\fP)
.TP
.B \fBssherrorhint\fP
.sp
A hint shown to the user in the case of SSH error (e.g.
\fBPlease see http://company/internalwiki/ssh.html\fP)
.TP
.B \fBstrict\fP
.sp
Require exact command names, instead of allowing unambiguous
abbreviations. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBstyle\fP
.sp
Name of style to use for command output.
.TP
.B \fBsupportcontact\fP
.sp
A URL where users should report a Mercurial traceback. Use this if you are a
large organisation with its own Mercurial deployment process and crash
reports should be addressed to your internal support.
.TP
.B \fBtextwidth\fP
.sp
Maximum width of help text. A longer line generated by \fBhg help\fP or
\fBhg subcommand \-\-help\fP will be broken after white space to get this
width or the terminal width, whichever comes first.
A non\-positive value will disable this and the terminal width will be
used. (default: 78)
.TP
.B \fBtimeout\fP
.sp
The timeout used when a lock is held (in seconds), a negative value
means no timeout. (default: 600)
.TP
.B \fBtimeout.warn\fP
.sp
Time (in seconds) before a warning is printed about held lock. A negative
value means no warning. (default: 0)
.TP
.B \fBtraceback\fP
.sp
Mercurial always prints a traceback when an unknown exception
occurs. Setting this to True will make Mercurial print a traceback
on all exceptions, even those recognized by Mercurial (such as
IOError or MemoryError). (default: False)
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBtweakdefaults\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
By default Mercurial\(aqs behavior changes very little from release
to release, but over time the recommended config settings
shift. Enable this config to opt in to get automatic tweaks to
Mercurial\(aqs behavior over time. This config setting will have no
effect if \fBHGPLAIN\fP is set or \fBHGPLAINEXCEPT\fP is set and does
not include \fBtweakdefaults\fP. (default: False)
.sp
It currently means:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[ui]
# The rollback command is dangerous. As a rule, don\(aqt use it.
rollback = False
# Make \(gahg status\(ga report copy information
statuscopies = yes
# Prefer curses UIs when available. Revert to plain\-text with \(gatext\(ga.
interface = curses
# Make compatible commands emit cwd\-relative paths by default.
relative\-paths = yes

[commands]
# Grep working directory by default.
grep.all\-files = True
# Refuse to perform an \(gahg update\(ga that would cause a file content merge
update.check = noconflict
# Show conflicts information in \(gahg status\(ga
status.verbose = True
# Make \(gahg resolve\(ga with no action (like \(ga\-m\(ga) fail instead of re\-merging.
resolve.explicit\-re\-merge = True

[diff]
git = 1
showfunc = 1
word\-diff = 1
.ft P
.fi
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBusername\fP
.sp
The committer of a changeset created when running "commit".
Typically a person\(aqs name and email address, e.g. \fBFred Widget
<fred@example.com>\fP. Environment variables in the
username are expanded.
.sp
(default: \fB$EMAIL\fP or \fBusername@hostname\fP. If the username in
hgrc is empty, e.g. if the system admin set \fBusername =\fP in the
system hgrc, it has to be specified manually or in a different
hgrc file)
.TP
.B \fBverbose\fP
.sp
Increase the amount of output printed. (default: False)
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBusage\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBrepository\-role\fP
.sp
What this repository is used for.
.sp
This is used to adjust behavior and performance to best fit the repository purpose.
.sp
Currently recognised values are:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBdefault\fP: an all purpose repository
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBresources\fP
.sp
How aggressive Mercurial can be with resource usage:
.sp
Currently recognised values are:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBdefault\fP: the default value currently is equivalent to medium,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBhigh\fP: allows for higher cpu, memory and disk\-space usage to improve
performance of some operations.
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBmedium\fP: aims at a moderate resource usage,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBlow\fP: reduces resources usage when possible, decreasing overall
performance.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For finer configuration, see also \fIusage.resources.cpu\fP,
\fIusage.resources.disk\fP and \fIusage.resources.memory\fP.
.TP
.B \fBresources.cpu\fP
.sp
How aggressive Mercurial can be in terms of cpu usage:
.sp
Currently recognised values are:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBdefault\fP: the default value, inherits the value from \fIusage.resources\fP,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBhigh\fP: allows for more aggressive cpu usage, improving storage quality
and the performance of some operations at the expense of machine load
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fImedium\fP:  aims at a moderate cpu usage,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fIlow\fP:  reduces cpu usage when possible, potentially at the expense of
slower operations, increased storage and exchange payload.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBresources.disk\fP
.sp
How aggressive Mercurial can be in terms of disk usage:
.sp
Currently recognised values are::
\- \fBdefault\fP: the default value, inherits the value from \fIusage.resources\fP,
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBhigh\fP: allows for more disk space usage where it can improve performance,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBmedium\fP:  aims at a moderate disk usage,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBlow\fP:  reduces disk usage when possible, decreasing performance in some
occasion.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBresources.memory\fP
.sp
How aggressive Mercurial can be in terms of memory usage:
.sp
Currently recognised values are:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
\- \(ga\(gadefault\(ga\(ga: the default value, inherits the value from \(gausage.resources\(ga,
.ft P
.fi
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBhigh\fP: allows for more aggressive memory usage to improve overall
performance,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBmedium\fP:  aims at a moderate memory usage,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBlow\fP: reduces memory usage when possible at the cost of overall
performance.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBresources.bandwidth\fP
.sp
How aggressive Mercurial can be in terms of bandwidth usage:
.sp
Currently recognised values are:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
\- \(ga\(gadefault\(ga\(ga: the default value, inherits the value from \(gausage.resources\(ga,
.ft P
.fi
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBhigh\fP: allows for more aggressive bandwidth usage to improve overall
performance,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBmedium\fP:  aims at a moderate bandwidth usage,
.IP \(bu 2
.
\fBlow\fP: reduces bandwidth usage when possible at the cost of overall
performance.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBcommand\-templates\fP
.sp
Templates used for customizing the output of commands.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBgraphnode\fP
.sp
The template used to print changeset nodes in an ASCII revision graph.
(default: \fB{graphnode}\fP)
.TP
.B \fBlog\fP
.sp
Template string for commands that print changesets.
.TP
.B \fBmergemarker\fP
.sp
The template used to print the commit description next to each conflict
marker during merge conflicts. See \%\fBhg help templates\fP\: for the template
format.
.sp
Defaults to showing the hash, tags, branches, bookmarks, author, and
the first line of the commit description.
.sp
If you use non\-ASCII characters in names for tags, branches, bookmarks,
authors, and/or commit descriptions, you must pay attention to encodings of
managed files. At template expansion, non\-ASCII characters use the encoding
specified by the \fB\-\-encoding\fP global option, \fBHGENCODING\fP or other
environment variables that govern your locale. If the encoding of the merge
markers is different from the encoding of the merged files,
serious problems may occur.
.sp
Can be overridden per\-merge\-tool, see the \fB[merge\-tools]\fP section.
.TP
.B \fBoneline\-summary\fP
.sp
A template used by \fIhg rebase\fP and other commands for showing a one\-line
summary of a commit. If the template configured here is longer than one
line, then only the first line is used.
.sp
The template can be overridden per command by defining a template in
\fIoneline\-summary.<command>\fP, where \fI<command>\fP can be e.g. "rebase".
.TP
.B \fBpre\-merge\-tool\-output\fP
.sp
A template that is printed before executing an external merge tool. This can
be used to print out additional context that might be useful to have during
the conflict resolution, such as the description of the various commits
involved or bookmarks/tags.
.sp
Additional information is available in the \fBlocal\(ga, \(ga\(gabase\fP, and \fBother\fP
dicts. For example: \fB{local.label}\fP, \fB{base.name}\fP, or
\fB{other.islink}\fP.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBweb\fP
.sp
Web interface configuration. The settings in this section apply to
both the builtin webserver (started by \%\fBhg serve\fP\:) and the script you
run through a webserver (\fBhgweb.cgi\fP and the derivatives for FastCGI
and WSGI).
.sp
The Mercurial webserver does no authentication (it does not prompt for
usernames and passwords to validate \fIwho\fP users are), but it does do
authorization (it grants or denies access for \fIauthenticated users\fP
based on settings in this section). You must either configure your
webserver to do authentication for you, or disable the authorization
checks.
.sp
For a quick setup in a trusted environment, e.g., a private LAN, where
you want it to accept pushes from anybody, you can use the following
command line:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
$ hg \-\-config web.allow\-push=* \-\-config web.push_ssl=False serve
.ft P
.fi
.sp
Note that this will allow anybody to push anything to the server and
that this should not be used for public servers.
.sp
The full set of options is:
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBaccesslog\fP
.sp
Where to output the access log. (default: stdout)
.TP
.B \fBaddress\fP
.sp
Interface address to bind to. (default: all)
.TP
.B \fBallow\-archive\fP
.sp
List of archive format (bz2, gz, zip) allowed for downloading.
(default: empty)
.TP
.B \fBallowbz2\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Whether to allow .tar.bz2 downloading of repository
revisions.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBallowgz\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Whether to allow .tar.gz downloading of repository
revisions.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBallow\-pull\fP
.sp
Whether to allow pulling from the repository. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBallow\-push\fP
.sp
Whether to allow pushing to the repository. If empty or not set,
pushing is not allowed. If the special value \fB*\fP, any remote
user can push, including unauthenticated users. Otherwise, the
remote user must have been authenticated, and the authenticated
user name must be present in this list. The contents of the
allow\-push list are examined after the deny_push list.
.TP
.B \fBallow_read\fP
.sp
If the user has not already been denied repository access due to
the contents of deny_read, this list determines whether to grant
repository access to the user. If this list is not empty, and the
user is unauthenticated or not present in the list, then access is
denied for the user. If the list is empty or not set, then access
is permitted to all users by default. Setting allow_read to the
special value \fB*\fP is equivalent to it not being set (i.e. access
is permitted to all users). The contents of the allow_read list are
examined after the deny_read list.
.TP
.B \fBallowzip\fP
.sp
(DEPRECATED) Whether to allow .zip downloading of repository
revisions. This feature creates temporary files.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBarchivesubrepos\fP
.sp
Whether to recurse into subrepositories when archiving.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBbaseurl\fP
.sp
Base URL to use when publishing URLs in other locations, so
third\-party tools like email notification hooks can construct
URLs. Example: \fBhttp://hgserver/repos/\fP.
.TP
.B \fBcacerts\fP
.sp
Path to file containing a list of PEM encoded certificate
authority certificates. Environment variables and \fB~user\fP
constructs are expanded in the filename. If specified on the
client, then it will verify the identity of remote HTTPS servers
with these certificates.
.sp
To disable SSL verification temporarily, specify \fB\-\-insecure\fP from
command line.
.sp
You can use OpenSSL\(aqs CA certificate file if your platform has
one. On most Linux systems this will be
\fB/etc/ssl/certs/ca\-certificates.crt\fP. Otherwise you will have to
generate this file manually. The form must be as follows:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
\&... (certificate in base64 PEM encoding) ...
\-\-\-\-\-END CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
\-\-\-\-\-BEGIN CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
\&... (certificate in base64 PEM encoding) ...
\-\-\-\-\-END CERTIFICATE\-\-\-\-\-
.ft P
.fi
.TP
.B \fBcache\fP
.sp
Whether to support caching in hgweb. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBcertificate\fP
.sp
Certificate to use when running \%\fBhg serve\fP\:.
.TP
.B \fBcollapse\fP
.sp
With \fBdescend\fP enabled, repositories in subdirectories are shown at
a single level alongside repositories in the current path. With
\fBcollapse\fP also enabled, repositories residing at a deeper level than
the current path are grouped behind navigable directory entries that
lead to the locations of these repositories. In effect, this setting
collapses each collection of repositories found within a subdirectory
into a single entry for that subdirectory. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBcomparisoncontext\fP
.sp
Number of lines of context to show in side\-by\-side file comparison. If
negative or the value \fBfull\fP, whole files are shown. (default: 5)
.sp
This setting can be overridden by a \fBcontext\fP request parameter to the
\fBcomparison\fP command, taking the same values.
.TP
.B \fBcontact\fP
.sp
Name or email address of the person in charge of the repository.
(default: ui.username or \fB$EMAIL\fP or "unknown" if unset or empty)
.TP
.B \fBcsp\fP
.sp
Send a \fBContent\-Security\-Policy\fP HTTP header with this value.
.sp
The value may contain a special string \fB%nonce%\fP, which will be replaced
by a randomly\-generated one\-time use value. If the value contains
\fB%nonce%\fP, \fBweb.cache\fP will be disabled, as caching undermines the
one\-time property of the nonce. This nonce will also be inserted into
\fB<script>\fP elements containing inline JavaScript.
.sp
Note: lots of HTML content sent by the server is derived from repository
data. Please consider the potential for malicious repository data to
"inject" itself into generated HTML content as part of your security
threat model.
.TP
.B \fBdeny_push\fP
.sp
Whether to deny pushing to the repository. If empty or not set,
push is not denied. If the special value \fB*\fP, all remote users are
denied push. Otherwise, unauthenticated users are all denied, and
any authenticated user name present in this list is also denied. The
contents of the deny_push list are examined before the allow\-push list.
.TP
.B \fBdeny_read\fP
.sp
Whether to deny reading/viewing of the repository. If this list is
not empty, unauthenticated users are all denied, and any
authenticated user name present in this list is also denied access to
the repository. If set to the special value \fB*\fP, all remote users
are denied access (rarely needed ;). If deny_read is empty or not set,
the determination of repository access depends on the presence and
content of the allow_read list (see description). If both
deny_read and allow_read are empty or not set, then access is
permitted to all users by default. If the repository is being
served via hgwebdir, denied users will not be able to see it in
the list of repositories. The contents of the deny_read list have
priority over (are examined before) the contents of the allow_read
list.
.TP
.B \fBdescend\fP
.sp
hgwebdir indexes will not descend into subdirectories. Only repositories
directly in the current path will be shown (other repositories are still
available from the index corresponding to their containing path).
.TP
.B \fBdescription\fP
.sp
Textual description of the repository\(aqs purpose or contents.
(default: "unknown")
.TP
.B \fBencoding\fP
.sp
Character encoding name. (default: the current locale charset)
Example: "UTF\-8".
.TP
.B \fBerrorlog\fP
.sp
Where to output the error log. (default: stderr)
.TP
.B \fBguessmime\fP
.sp
Control MIME types for raw download of file content.
Set to True to let hgweb guess the content type from the file
extension. This will serve HTML files as \fBtext/html\fP and might
allow cross\-site scripting attacks when serving untrusted
repositories. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBhidden\fP
.sp
Whether to hide the repository in the hgwebdir index.
(default: False)
.TP
.B \fBipv6\fP
.sp
Whether to use IPv6. (default: False)
.TP
.B \fBlabels\fP
.sp
List of string \fIlabels\fP associated with the repository.
.sp
Labels are exposed as a template keyword and can be used to customize
output. e.g. the \fBindex\fP template can group or filter repositories
by labels and the \fBsummary\fP template can display additional content
if a specific label is present.
.TP
.B \fBlogoimg\fP
.sp
File name of the logo image that some templates display on each page.
The file name is relative to \fBstaticurl\fP. That is, the full path to
the logo image is "staticurl/logoimg".
If unset, \fBhglogo.png\fP will be used.
.TP
.B \fBlogourl\fP
.sp
Base URL to use for logos. If unset, \fBhttps://mercurial\-scm.org/\fP
will be used.
.TP
.B \fBmaxchanges\fP
.sp
Maximum number of changes to list on the changelog. (default: 10)
.TP
.B \fBmaxfiles\fP
.sp
Maximum number of files to list per changeset. (default: 10)
.TP
.B \fBmaxshortchanges\fP
.sp
Maximum number of changes to list on the shortlog, graph or filelog
pages. (default: 60)
.TP
.B \fBname\fP
.sp
Repository name to use in the web interface.
(default: current working directory)
.TP
.B \fBport\fP
.sp
Port to listen on. (default: 8000)
.TP
.B \fBprefix\fP
.sp
Prefix path to serve from. (default: \(aq\(aq (server root))
.TP
.B \fBpush_ssl\fP
.sp
Whether to require that inbound pushes be transported over SSL to
prevent password sniffing. (default: True)
.TP
.B \fBrefreshinterval\fP
.sp
How frequently directory listings re\-scan the filesystem for new
repositories, in seconds. This is relevant when wildcards are used
to define paths. Depending on how much filesystem traversal is
required, refreshing may negatively impact performance.
.sp
Values less than or equal to 0 always refresh.
(default: 20)
.TP
.B \fBserver\-header\fP
.sp
Value for HTTP \fBServer\fP response header.
.TP
.B \fBstatic\fP
.sp
Directory where static files are served from.
.TP
.B \fBstaticurl\fP
.sp
Base URL to use for static files. If unset, static files (e.g. the
hgicon.png favicon) will be served by the CGI script itself. Use
this setting to serve them directly with the HTTP server.
Example: \fBhttp://hgserver/static/\fP.
.TP
.B \fBstripes\fP
.sp
How many lines a "zebra stripe" should span in multi\-line output.
Set to 0 to disable. (default: 1)
.TP
.B \fBstyle\fP
.sp
Which template map style to use. The available options are the names of
subdirectories in the HTML templates path. (default: \fBpaper\fP)
Example: \fBmonoblue\fP.
.TP
.B \fBtemplates\fP
.sp
Where to find the HTML templates. The default path to the HTML templates
can be obtained from \fBhg debuginstall\fP.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBwebsub\fP
.sp
Web substitution filter definition. You can use this section to
define a set of regular expression substitution patterns which
let you automatically modify the hgweb server output.
.sp
The default hgweb templates only apply these substitution patterns
on the revision description fields. You can apply them anywhere
you want when you create your own templates by adding calls to the
"websub" filter (usually after calling the "escape" filter).
.sp
This can be used, for example, to convert issue references to links
to your issue tracker, or to convert "markdown\-like" syntax into
HTML (see the examples below).
.sp
Each entry in this section names a substitution filter.
The value of each entry defines the substitution expression itself.
The websub expressions follow the old interhg extension syntax,
which in turn imitates the Unix sed replacement syntax:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
patternname = s/SEARCH_REGEX/REPLACE_EXPRESSION/[i]
.ft P
.fi
.sp
You can use any separator other than "/". The final "i" is optional
and indicates that the search must be case insensitive.
.sp
Examples:
.sp
.nf
.ft C
[websub]
issues = s|issue(\ed+)|<a href="http://bts.example.org/issue\e1">issue\e1</a>|i
italic = s/\eb_(\eS+)_\eb/<i>\e1<\e/i>/
bold = s/\e*\eb(\eS+)\eb\e*/<b>\e1<\e/b>/
.ft P
.fi
.SS \fBworker\fP
.sp
Parallel master/worker configuration. We currently perform working
directory updates in parallel on Unix\-like systems, which greatly
helps performance.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B \fBenabled\fP
.sp
Whether to enable workers code to be used.
(default: true)
.TP
.B \fBnumcpus\fP
.sp
Number of CPUs to use for parallel operations. A zero or
negative value is treated as \fBuse the default\fP.
(default: 4 or the number of CPUs on the system, whichever is larger)
.TP
.B \fBbackgroundclose\fP
.sp
Whether to enable closing file handles on background threads during certain
operations. Some platforms aren\(aqt very efficient at closing file
handles that have been written or appended to. By performing file closing
on background threads, file write rate can increase substantially.
(default: true on Windows, false elsewhere)
.TP
.B \fBbackgroundcloseminfilecount\fP
.sp
Minimum number of files required to trigger background file closing.
Operations not writing this many files won\(aqt start background close
threads.
(default: 2048)
.TP
.B \fBbackgroundclosemaxqueue\fP
.sp
The maximum number of opened file handles waiting to be closed in the
background. This option only has an effect if \fBbackgroundclose\fP is
enabled.
(default: 384)
.TP
.B \fBbackgroundclosethreadcount\fP
.sp
Number of threads to process background file closes. Only relevant if
\fBbackgroundclose\fP is enabled.
(default: 4)
.TP
.B \fBparallel\-stream\-bundle\-processing\fP
.sp
Read, parse and write stream bundle in parallel speeding up the process.
.sp
Also see \fIworker.parallel\-stream\-bundle\-processing.memory\-target\fP to control
the memory usage of the buffers.
.sp
Also see \fIworker.parallel\-stream\-bundle\-processing.num\-writer\fP to control the
amount of concurrent writers.
.TP
.B \fBparallel\-stream\-bundle\-processing.memory\-target\fP
.sp
Limit memory usage around this value. This is not a hard target a Mercurial will use a bit more, but this reduce the amount of buffered stream content that we read ahead.
.sp
If set to a negative value, the amount of memory used will not be restricted.
.sp
When set to 0, the default value depends on the value of the
\fIusage.resources.memory\fP configuration:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
low:    100 MB
.IP \(bu 2
.
medium: 500 MB (default)
.IP \(bu 2
.
high:     2 GB
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B \fBparallel\-stream\-bundle\-processing.num\-writer\fP
.sp
Control the number of file being written concurrently when applying a stream
bundle.
.sp
When set to 0, the default, the value used depends on the value of the
\fIusage.resources.cpu\fP configuration:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
.
low:    1 writer
.IP \(bu 2
.
medium: 4 writers (default)
.IP \(bu 2
.
high:   8 writers
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
